WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.968 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.936 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:07.380 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:17.060 align:middle line:90%


00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:22.040 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: All right, I want to
welcome Stefan Andreev as guest

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:22.900 align:middle line:90%
lecture today.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:28.400 align:middle line:84%
He has been a significant part
of this course over the past,

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.620 align:middle line:90%
I think, 11 years.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.880 align:middle line:90%
Is that right, Stefan?

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.260 align:middle line:90%
STEFAN ANDREEV: A long period.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.700 align:middle line:90%
And I can't count that far.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.960 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Partly because his
lectures are the most popular

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.720 align:middle line:84%
in the course, and we
really are glad to have

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.320 align:middle line:90%
him come back again.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.880 align:middle line:84%
His background is
very interesting.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.840 align:middle line:84%
He has a PhD in Chemical
Physics from Harvard.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.480 align:middle line:84%
And he has worked
at the top firms

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:01.970 align:middle line:84%
on Wall Street, Morgan Stanley,
Citadel, and now to Sigma.

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:07.110 align:middle line:84%
So he's a really great person
to share his expertise with us.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.570 align:middle line:90%
So thank you.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:10.090 align:middle line:90%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Thank you.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:10.750 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.290 align:middle line:90%
Thank you, guys.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.850 align:middle line:90%
Very nice to meet you all.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.373 align:middle line:90%
Big class, big class this year.

00:01:18.373 --> 00:01:19.040 align:middle line:90%
That's exciting.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.170 align:middle line:90%


00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.770 align:middle line:84%
I'll start with this before
everybody gets tired.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.170 align:middle line:90%
I wanted to say that like I--

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.450 align:middle line:84%
we're going to talk about
principal component analysis

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:29.810 align:middle line:90%
today.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:35.570 align:middle line:84%
And some of the main things I
want you to take away from this

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:41.370 align:middle line:84%
are this is a absolutely core
data science tool in finance.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:42.530 align:middle line:90%
That's number one.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:46.410 align:middle line:90%
Number two, it's great to--

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:50.410 align:middle line:84%
this kind of stuff, you
only learn by doing it,

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:55.650 align:middle line:84%
so actually doing
some kind of project

00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:00.410 align:middle line:84%
where you use this toolkit
is essential for you

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:03.290 align:middle line:90%
to really learn it.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:07.730 align:middle line:84%
And three, if you ever want
to get a job in finance,

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:12.650 align:middle line:84%
I think having done
a project with this

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.410 align:middle line:84%
and having some familiarity
with what this is about

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:18.570 align:middle line:84%
might help you during
interviews quite a bit.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:21.370 align:middle line:84%
We're going to have
lectures and slides.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:23.996 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to have some--
the slides are on the web,

00:02:23.996 --> 00:02:27.290 align:middle line:90%
on the classroom website.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:32.530 align:middle line:84%
In addition to that,
there is a notebook,

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.130 align:middle line:84%
and a Jupyter
Notebook and a data

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:39.727 align:middle line:84%
set that I've used to prepare
these slides, at least

00:02:39.727 --> 00:02:41.560 align:middle line:84%
the first example we're
going to talk about.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.530 align:middle line:90%


00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.290 align:middle line:84%
Like I think that's
a great reference

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:53.130 align:middle line:84%
if you ever want to see how
this stuff gets computed.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:58.260 align:middle line:84%
And if you want to do like
a project, final project

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:05.080 align:middle line:84%
in the class, it would be
something like that notebook,

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:06.880 align:middle line:90%
but a little more elaborate.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.580 align:middle line:84%
So it gives you a
little bit of a flavor

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.380 align:middle line:84%
of what the starting point
and what the project would

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.980 align:middle line:84%
be like, probably be a little
more extensive than what

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:22.150 align:middle line:84%
we're talking about here,
but like in the same spirit.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:24.780 align:middle line:90%


00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.820 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.580 align:middle line:84%
Now that we got this over with,
let's talk about what is PCA

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:35.780 align:middle line:84%
and where does it fit within
the world of machine learning

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:37.340 align:middle line:90%
algorithms.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:41.300 align:middle line:84%
And it's basically--
it's a method

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:42.960 align:middle line:90%
for unsupervised learning.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.980 align:middle line:84%
So it's basically a
method to cluster,

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.070 align:middle line:84%
reduce dimensionality to
make sense of the data.

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:54.410 align:middle line:90%


00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:56.270 align:middle line:90%
It's not a regression.

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.330 align:middle line:84%
There's no causality
implied when you use PCA.

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:01.190 align:middle line:90%


00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.730 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it's a way to
understand data better,

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:06.870 align:middle line:84%
especially data that
has a lot of dimensions,

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:12.230 align:middle line:84%
where many of those dimensions
are highly correlated.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:15.510 align:middle line:84%
Here I've listed a
bunch of examples

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:18.070 align:middle line:90%
of where PCA can be applied.

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:23.950 align:middle line:84%
Generally, it's widely
applied in large data

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:28.670 align:middle line:84%
sets, multi-dimensional
data, like a lot of data.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:31.210 align:middle line:84%
The actual calculations
are mostly linear algebra.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:33.690 align:middle line:84%
So it's efficient, and
fast, and scalable.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:39.862 align:middle line:84%
And this is one of the reasons
people generally like to use it.

00:04:39.862 --> 00:04:41.570 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to go
through all of these.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:44.310 align:middle line:90%


00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:48.270 align:middle line:90%
Their applications vary widely--

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.880 align:middle line:90%
they vary across science.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:57.200 align:middle line:84%
And in finance, it's used
very extensively as well.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.240 align:middle line:84%
So first thing I
wanted to talk about

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:04.963 align:middle line:84%
is go through a
visual example of--

00:05:04.963 --> 00:05:06.880 align:middle line:84%
some of these things
that I want to talk about

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.680 align:middle line:84%
are like my mental toy
models that I always

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.600 align:middle line:84%
refer to when I think about what
is happening when I'm using PCA.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:20.840 align:middle line:84%
In this example--
you guys see well?

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:22.360 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:27.460 align:middle line:84%
In this example, I've prepared
effectively three data sets.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.260 align:middle line:84%
But they're really
like one data set.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:31.352 align:middle line:90%
I've just made this up.

00:05:31.352 --> 00:05:32.060 align:middle line:90%
There's just one.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:34.560 align:middle line:84%
It's like
one-dimensional data set.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.860 align:middle line:84%
And all I've done is I've taken
these one-dimensional data set,

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:42.400 align:middle line:84%
and I've made it two
dimensional by just

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.360 align:middle line:84%
adding an extra coordinate
like the blue line.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.800 align:middle line:84%
The extra coordinate
y, I made 0.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.680 align:middle line:84%
And then I took this
data set and I just

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:53.440 align:middle line:84%
rotated it like 45 degrees
and then 90 degrees.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:59.680 align:middle line:84%
And in this two-dimensional
world, it's two sides.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:00.980 align:middle line:90%
Each point has two coordinates.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:02.600 align:middle line:84%
So it looks like
it's two dimensional.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:07.500 align:middle line:84%
But I know it's one
dimensional because I made it.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.640 align:middle line:84%
And the key thing
that PCA would do

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.240 align:middle line:84%
is PCA would immediately tell
you, when it looks at this data,

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.240 align:middle line:84%
that it's actually
one dimensional.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.040 align:middle line:90%
You download it.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:20.165 align:middle line:90%
You do the calculation.

00:06:20.165 --> 00:06:21.540 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be
one dimensional.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.250 align:middle line:90%
So how would it work?

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:27.680 align:middle line:90%


00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.650 align:middle line:84%
You can think of PCA as a way
to rotate the coordinate system.

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:36.680 align:middle line:90%


00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:40.212 align:middle line:84%
Basically when you apply PCA
to this, it will tell you--

00:06:40.212 --> 00:06:42.180 align:middle line:84%
if you apply it
to the blue data,

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:47.730 align:middle line:84%
it will tell you that the PC1,
the first principal component--

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:50.350 align:middle line:84%
Peter, you guys have done
the math part already of PCA?

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:50.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:54.710 align:middle line:84%
So the first principal component
is going to be the x direction.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:56.210 align:middle line:84%
When you do the red
line, it's going

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.950 align:middle line:84%
to tell you that it's
like the 1, 1 direction.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:07.870 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to just discover
this direction dimensionality.

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:09.610 align:middle line:84%
The first data set
was very, very simple

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:12.370 align:middle line:84%
because it's purely
one dimensional.

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:14.610 align:middle line:84%
This one here is the
same thing, but I've

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:18.530 align:middle line:84%
added a little bit of noise
in the other dimension.

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:22.230 align:middle line:84%
And now when I
apply PCA to this,

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:24.310 align:middle line:84%
it's going to tell me
basically the same thing.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:27.490 align:middle line:84%
PC1 is going to be
the same, but PC2

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:29.250 align:middle line:84%
is going to have a
little bit of variance

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:31.910 align:middle line:84%
along PC2, along the
perpendicular direction.

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:34.970 align:middle line:84%
So in both of these
cases, A and B,

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:39.330 align:middle line:84%
it will tell me that PC1 and
PC2 eigenvectors are the same.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:41.250 align:middle line:84%
And what's going
to be different is

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:43.810 align:middle line:84%
there's going to be
0 variance in case

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:49.140 align:middle line:84%
of B on the PC2 direction
and a little bit of variance

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:54.060 align:middle line:84%
in example A, along
the PC2 direction.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.030 align:middle line:90%
So it basically tells us--

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:58.780 align:middle line:84%
the two things it tells
us, the directions

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.820 align:middle line:84%
along which the data is
concentrated and the variance

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:07.350 align:middle line:84%
or how important that direction
is on a relative basis.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:13.435 align:middle line:90%


00:08:13.435 --> 00:08:15.060 align:middle line:84%
What's nice about
this-- like I've done

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.140 align:middle line:90%
this two-dimensional plots.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:21.820 align:middle line:84%
But PCA, like when you actually
do it, it's just linear algebra.

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:24.100 align:middle line:90%
And you can do it on--

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:26.740 align:middle line:84%
you can do it on any
number of dimensions.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:28.300 align:middle line:90%
You can do it on--

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:29.060 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:32.039 align:middle line:84%
So when you have
high dimensionality,

00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:36.260 align:middle line:84%
this is a very robust
and powerful tool.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:39.179 align:middle line:84%
What's really nice about that
in the high dimensionality

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:41.240 align:middle line:90%
is like it's not a minimization.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:43.059 align:middle line:90%
It's not an optimization.

00:08:43.059 --> 00:08:45.380 align:middle line:84%
It's just a linear
algebra calculation.

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:47.320 align:middle line:90%
And it's fast.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:50.910 align:middle line:84%
So you can process very large
amounts of data very quickly.

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:56.100 align:middle line:90%


00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:02.200 align:middle line:84%
A very common-- like when
you use this in practice,

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:03.700 align:middle line:84%
like the answers
that you get from--

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:09.260 align:middle line:84%
the things you can learn
from PCA are going to be--

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:12.280 align:middle line:84%
it tells you a relationship
between variables.

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:15.800 align:middle line:84%
Like in our first example, if
you look at this diagonal line,

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:18.260 align:middle line:90%
it's going to tell you that--

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.160 align:middle line:84%
like y equals x is
kind of the line.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.540 align:middle line:84%
And then you can think, well,
I can discover the same thing

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:28.000 align:middle line:90%
if I regress y on x.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.282 align:middle line:84%
And then it's going to tell
me pretty much like the same--

00:09:31.282 --> 00:09:32.740 align:middle line:84%
if I look at my
diagonal line, it's

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:35.140 align:middle line:90%
going to tell me the same thing.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:38.220 align:middle line:84%
And that is true in the
one-dimensional example.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:40.140 align:middle line:84%
But I wanted to
give an illustration

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:45.260 align:middle line:84%
of what is the difference
between regression and PCA?

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:47.816 align:middle line:90%


00:09:47.816 --> 00:09:54.410 align:middle line:84%
Remember, PCA is-- remember,
it's unsupervised learning.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:55.850 align:middle line:90%
So there's no causality implied.

00:09:55.850 --> 00:09:59.307 align:middle line:84%
So x and y are kind of
on the same footing.

00:09:59.307 --> 00:10:00.890 align:middle line:84%
Each dimension is
on the same footing.

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:02.830 align:middle line:84%
There's no special
dimension that's dependent

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:04.430 align:middle line:90%
and one that is independent.

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:07.250 align:middle line:84%
So when you regress, this
is like the same data set.

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:08.990 align:middle line:84%
And you can see
here the regression

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:13.190 align:middle line:84%
of y versus x, the
regression of x versus y.

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:17.430 align:middle line:84%
And the PC1 line,
they're similar.

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:20.550 align:middle line:90%
But they're not the same.

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:22.430 align:middle line:90%
Like it's actually optimizing--

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:24.370 align:middle line:84%
it's minimizing the
distance to the line.

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:27.910 align:middle line:90%


00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:30.767 align:middle line:84%
This is minimizing the distance
in the perpendicular direction.

00:10:30.767 --> 00:10:32.350 align:middle line:84%
And the others are
minimizing distance

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:34.090 align:middle line:84%
in the independent
variable direction,

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:36.090 align:middle line:90%
so when you do a regression.

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:37.490 align:middle line:90%
So it's not exactly the same.

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:39.560 align:middle line:84%
I think it's good to
keep that in mind.

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:45.800 align:middle line:84%
Because sometimes if you
really want to understand,

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:50.360 align:middle line:84%
given an observation x,
how I should model y,

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:52.620 align:middle line:84%
maybe PCA is not
the right approach.

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:55.640 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you should
run a regression.

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:59.280 align:middle line:84%
But if you want to understand
the correlation structure of x

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.160 align:middle line:84%
and y, of two
variables, there is

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:04.660 align:middle line:84%
no-- you don't have necessarily
one depends on the other.

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:07.100 align:middle line:84%
You don't know anything
about their relationship.

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:10.180 align:middle line:84%
Then PCA is probably a
more appropriate tool.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:15.600 align:middle line:90%


00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:18.080 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so I was just
saying, if there

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:20.680 align:middle line:90%
is some data that is in some--

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:24.640 align:middle line:84%
if data is in a
particular line, PC1,

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:26.220 align:middle line:84%
which is the red
direction here, you

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:28.260 align:middle line:84%
will always discover
where that direction is.

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:32.400 align:middle line:84%
And if you actually map the
data into PC coordinates,

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:35.640 align:middle line:84%
it's just going to look like
a straight line like that.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:38.850 align:middle line:84%
And a couple of things that
I like about this example

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:43.690 align:middle line:84%
is you can see how rotating
the data set doesn't change

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:45.430 align:middle line:90%
the PC coordinate output.

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:48.390 align:middle line:84%
So it finds the
direction appropriately.

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:51.570 align:middle line:84%
The second thing that's very
important is robustness.

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:53.570 align:middle line:90%
In the real world, when you do--

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:56.610 align:middle line:84%
it could be in
science or in finance.

00:11:56.610 --> 00:11:58.750 align:middle line:84%
You're not going to
have perfect data.

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:00.130 align:middle line:90%
Everything is very noisy.

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:02.690 align:middle line:90%
And noise is a huge problem.

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:05.550 align:middle line:84%
And robustness to data
noise is very important.

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.830 align:middle line:84%
So this illustrates
how robust things are.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:15.050 align:middle line:84%
So if I take one point
and I just move it around,

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:19.730 align:middle line:84%
you can see that the red
line, which is the direction

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:22.410 align:middle line:90%
of my PC1, doesn't--

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:24.930 align:middle line:90%
it's quite robust.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:28.750 align:middle line:84%
It's quite robust to
noise, to one particular.

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:31.970 align:middle line:84%
If everything else is aligned,
just moving around one data,

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:35.250 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't really
change things too much.

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:40.210 align:middle line:84%
If I make them a little
bit more spread out,

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:44.690 align:middle line:84%
it's a little less robust,
but it's still pretty robust.

00:12:44.690 --> 00:12:46.270 align:middle line:84%
And the thing that
I wanted to say--

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:47.450 align:middle line:90%
what happened?

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:50.890 align:middle line:84%
People are just going to-- very
often, you just take some data

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:52.930 align:middle line:90%
and you just shove it into PCA.

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:55.893 align:middle line:84%
And you don't actually care
too much before you even

00:12:55.893 --> 00:12:56.810 align:middle line:90%
know what the data is.

00:12:56.810 --> 00:12:58.110 align:middle line:90%
And you just put it into PCA.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:01.050 align:middle line:84%
And PCA always
gives you something.

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:02.610 align:middle line:84%
But suppose the
data doesn't really

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:05.770 align:middle line:90%
have a dominant direction.

00:13:05.770 --> 00:13:09.610 align:middle line:84%
Then the PCA algorithm
becomes not robust.

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:10.487 align:middle line:90%
So you can see now.

00:13:10.487 --> 00:13:11.570 align:middle line:90%
Look, I'm moving one data.

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:13.850 align:middle line:84%
And look what's happening
to the coordinate.

00:13:13.850 --> 00:13:15.850 align:middle line:90%
One data point is--

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:17.330 align:middle line:84%
I can just move
that a little bit,

00:13:17.330 --> 00:13:19.770 align:middle line:84%
and it's just
completely giving me--

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:24.330 align:middle line:84%
the output is super sensitive
to any noise in the data.

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:27.290 align:middle line:90%
So it's worthless.

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:30.810 align:middle line:84%
So it's very important when
we're looking at the data

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:34.027 align:middle line:84%
if we don't really know
what this data looks like.

00:13:34.027 --> 00:13:35.360 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes it's high-dimensional.

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:37.380 align:middle line:84%
You can't even
really visualize it.

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:41.700 align:middle line:84%
You have to think, how do I
understand whether there's

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:44.460 align:middle line:84%
actual structure in the data
that I'm trying to discover?

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:46.300 align:middle line:90%
This will help me discover--

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:47.663 align:middle line:90%
quantify the structure.

00:13:47.663 --> 00:13:49.580 align:middle line:84%
But it's also good to
understand qualitatively

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:51.080 align:middle line:90%
how much the structure is.

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:54.540 align:middle line:90%


00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:57.380 align:middle line:90%
One thing that PCA also--

00:13:57.380 --> 00:13:59.546 align:middle line:84%
part of the PCA
analysis at the output--

00:13:59.546 --> 00:14:00.046 align:middle line:90%
yes?

00:14:00.046 --> 00:14:04.900 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, how many
is [INAUDIBLE] making it

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:07.900 align:middle line:84%
around the parts to
see how robust it

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:10.660 align:middle line:84%
is, to see if the data is
actually close to the line

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:11.570 align:middle line:90%
or not?

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:13.490 align:middle line:90%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Well, it's--

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:16.300 align:middle line:90%


00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:18.080 align:middle line:90%
no, but what--

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:20.460 align:middle line:84%
I don't have-- you can
write tools to do that.

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:23.260 align:middle line:84%
But I think what
you want-- one thing

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:26.500 align:middle line:84%
that you can do that
is very important when

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:31.140 align:middle line:84%
you do your PCA analysis is
to look at the eigenvalues.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:33.510 align:middle line:84%
Because very often, people
look at the eigenvectors.

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:34.930 align:middle line:84%
And they're like, oh,
these are my directions.

00:14:34.930 --> 00:14:36.597 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to
project things, and I'm

00:14:36.597 --> 00:14:40.470 align:middle line:84%
going to assume everything
is factorized nicely.

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:43.250 align:middle line:84%
And we don't really look
as much at the eigenvalues,

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:45.750 align:middle line:84%
but it's very important to
look at the eigenvalues.

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:50.590 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is that in
this example, when there's

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:53.430 align:middle line:84%
no dominant direction,
the eigenvalues, which

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:55.990 align:middle line:84%
measure the variance
along each direction,

00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:57.250 align:middle line:90%
would be very similar.

00:14:57.250 --> 00:15:00.270 align:middle line:84%
So when you look
at-- when you're

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:04.030 align:middle line:84%
using a principal component like
the first principal component,

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:07.990 align:middle line:84%
you want to make sure that the
eigenvalue of that component

00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:12.070 align:middle line:84%
you're using is quite
distinct from the rest

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:14.710 align:middle line:90%
of the eigenvalues.

00:15:14.710 --> 00:15:16.670 align:middle line:84%
In finance, we'll
see later we're

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:18.710 align:middle line:84%
dealing with the
time series of PCA.

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:21.270 align:middle line:84%
Every day, you
construct a PCA model.

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:24.030 align:middle line:84%
So anything that's a
number here, the eigenvalue

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:25.970 align:middle line:84%
is actually a time
series of eigenvalue.

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:29.430 align:middle line:84%
So the thing I look at a
lot when I'm doing analysis

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:33.680 align:middle line:84%
is I look at eigenvalue
plots in log space.

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:39.880 align:middle line:84%
And I want to see that each
distinct component that I use--

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:42.000 align:middle line:84%
ideally, you want
to see that it's

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:48.560 align:middle line:84%
an order of magnitude different
than the next one in variance,

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:52.460 align:middle line:84%
basically, which if
that's true, that's great.

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:54.540 align:middle line:90%
And that doesn't always happen.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:55.873 align:middle line:90%
But at least you want it to be--

00:15:55.873 --> 00:15:57.415 align:middle line:84%
visually, you want
it to be distinct.

00:15:57.415 --> 00:15:58.700 align:middle line:90%
You want it to be different.

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:02.280 align:middle line:84%
You don't want-- if you have
a time series of things,

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:04.900 align:middle line:84%
you don't want the first
and the second one to cross.

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:08.040 align:middle line:84%
And if that's happening, then
the first and second principal

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:11.520 align:middle line:84%
component-- if the
eigenvalues are small changes

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:14.440 align:middle line:84%
or causing them
to switch places,

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:16.600 align:middle line:84%
it basically means there
isn't a lot of structure.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:19.240 align:middle line:84%
And the PCA output is
noisy, and it's actually

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:22.600 align:middle line:84%
going to obfuscate
rather than clarify

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:24.440 align:middle line:90%
the thing you're looking at.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:27.200 align:middle line:84%
I've put here a little
bit of math notation.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:30.680 align:middle line:84%
You guys have done your
math lecture on PCA,

00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:34.120 align:middle line:84%
so we can be
relatively quick here.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:37.400 align:middle line:90%
But I do want to go through it.

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:42.520 align:middle line:84%
The main thing is
you have your data.

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:46.600 align:middle line:84%
Data matrix is
generally-- there's

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:49.100 align:middle line:90%
n observations in p dimensions.

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:50.880 align:middle line:84%
And we think of
that as a matrix.

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:57.120 align:middle line:84%
And then if this matrix is x,
then every day's observation are

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:00.360 align:middle line:90%
vectors of size p.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:06.040 align:middle line:84%
And then you have a covariance
matrix, which is XT times X.

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:13.079 align:middle line:90%
And then there is--

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:19.480 align:middle line:84%
I've here defined the wk,
or the orthonormal vectors

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:21.900 align:middle line:84%
representing the-- these
are the eigenvectors.

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:22.400 align:middle line:90%
Wk.

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:24.440 align:middle line:84%
And then I've
written down the math

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:29.610 align:middle line:84%
of how you do the projection of
data onto principal components

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:33.770 align:middle line:84%
and how you compute
the variance.

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:35.230 align:middle line:90%
How do you get these vectors?

00:17:35.230 --> 00:17:40.530 align:middle line:90%
Well, there is-- you could do--

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:43.010 align:middle line:84%
you could take your data,
compute covariance matrix,

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:47.410 align:middle line:84%
feed it into an eigenvalue
calculator, which is basically--

00:17:47.410 --> 00:17:51.810 align:middle line:84%
in my work, our
data is not really--

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:54.070 align:middle line:84%
the number of dimensions
is not actually that high.

00:17:54.070 --> 00:17:56.850 align:middle line:90%
So that's pretty-- that's OK.

00:17:56.850 --> 00:18:02.710 align:middle line:84%
It does scale, as that
thing is potentially

00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:05.610 align:middle line:84%
maybe a little bit slow, but
it's not actually that slow.

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:08.290 align:middle line:84%
There is also a more
efficient method

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:11.990 align:middle line:84%
called SVD, Singular
Value Decomposition,

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:16.010 align:middle line:84%
where you can just compute your
projections without calculating

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:18.123 align:middle line:90%
the covariance matrix.

00:18:18.123 --> 00:18:19.790 align:middle line:84%
I like to compute the
covariance matrix,

00:18:19.790 --> 00:18:22.450 align:middle line:84%
because we generally need to
compute the covariance matrix

00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:23.870 align:middle line:90%
for other things in any case.

00:18:23.870 --> 00:18:29.180 align:middle line:84%
So if you have it
already, then it's--

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:30.480 align:middle line:90%
you might as well use it.

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:35.620 align:middle line:90%


00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:37.580 align:middle line:84%
So there's some--
I've listed here

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:41.020 align:middle line:84%
some properties of
this eigenvector matrix

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:44.880 align:middle line:84%
that are important,
that it's orthonormal,

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:49.740 align:middle line:84%
which means that the transpose
of the eigenvector matrix

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:52.260 align:middle line:90%
is also an inverse.

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:55.800 align:middle line:84%
The covariance matrices
can be written as the--

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:58.720 align:middle line:84%
in this form, where lambda
is the eigenvalue matrix.

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:01.100 align:middle line:90%
That's the SVD decomposition.

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:04.040 align:middle line:84%
There's total variance
quantity you can think about,

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:05.700 align:middle line:84%
which is the
variance of x, which

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:07.320 align:middle line:90%
is just the trace of lambda.

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:13.340 align:middle line:84%
So the product of eigenvalues is
the variance of your data set.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:17.900 align:middle line:84%
And there is something called
percent variance explained,

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:21.740 align:middle line:84%
which is just defined
as how much variance

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:25.300 align:middle line:84%
is along a particular
PC direction divided

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:26.860 align:middle line:90%
by the total variance.

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:32.228 align:middle line:84%
So that adds up to
100% when you're--

00:19:32.228 --> 00:19:35.140 align:middle line:84%
but that's very useful
because if you remember

00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:37.740 align:middle line:84%
how I was saying want to
look at the eigenvalues

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:41.620 align:middle line:84%
to understand how important
each direction is,

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:44.380 align:middle line:84%
variance explained tells
you that as a number.

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:47.580 align:middle line:84%
You want to look at--
you want to-- when you're

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:51.220 align:middle line:84%
trying to model your data
set with as few dimensions as

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:54.980 align:middle line:84%
possible, you want to
add more PC directions

00:19:54.980 --> 00:19:59.020 align:middle line:84%
until you get to a relatively
high variance explained.

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:01.420 align:middle line:90%
If you're able to explain--

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:03.040 align:middle line:84%
if you have a
10-dimensional data set,

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:07.120 align:middle line:84%
and you're able to explain 80%
of it using three dimensions,

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:08.920 align:middle line:90%
that's a pretty big win.

00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:11.780 align:middle line:90%


00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:16.380 align:middle line:84%
And I've also written out
this out-of-sample variance

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:17.000 align:middle line:90%
explained.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:20.000 align:middle line:84%
Or very often, what we
do is we calculate a PCA.

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:22.030 align:middle line:84%
We calibrate it on
historical data,

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:26.814 align:middle line:84%
and then we apply
it to future data

00:20:26.814 --> 00:20:33.210 align:middle line:84%
that we're trying to model
what will happen in the future.

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:35.470 align:middle line:84%
Especially in
finance we do that.

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:41.830 align:middle line:84%
So when the data you're
calibrating your PCA on

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:46.310 align:middle line:84%
is different than the
other data, then this--

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:49.410 align:middle line:84%
you hope that the structure of
the two data sets are the same.

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:51.070 align:middle line:84%
So if the structure
is the same, you'll

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:53.650 align:middle line:84%
get the same variance
explained structure.

00:20:53.650 --> 00:20:57.770 align:middle line:84%
But to the extent that
it's not true, you may not.

00:20:57.770 --> 00:21:04.150 align:middle line:84%
So you may see that your
dominance of certain PC1 factors

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:04.670 align:middle line:90%
becomes--

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:06.190 align:middle line:84%
it starts to explain
less variance

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:09.550 align:middle line:84%
as your out-of-sample
data becomes

00:21:09.550 --> 00:21:12.350 align:middle line:84%
more different from the
data, which you calibrate.

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:15.910 align:middle line:84%
So this is a little bit of
the modeling part of it,

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:21.100 align:middle line:84%
where going to be using some
data to calibrate the model.

00:21:21.100 --> 00:21:22.720 align:middle line:84%
And then you're
going to use this

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:28.480 align:middle line:84%
to predict the variance, the
correlation structure of what

00:21:28.480 --> 00:21:30.360 align:middle line:84%
happens in the future,
like future market

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:31.510 align:middle line:90%
moves or future returns.

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:35.480 align:middle line:90%


00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:43.760 align:middle line:84%
So a typical-- what do we
generally do when we apply PCA?

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:45.300 align:middle line:84%
We're going to
take our data set.

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:47.640 align:middle line:90%
We're going to demean it.

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:52.840 align:middle line:84%
And then maybe we're going to
normalize the variance to 1.

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:55.170 align:middle line:84%
When I say "maybe"-- and
the reason this "maybe" is--

00:21:55.170 --> 00:21:56.420 align:middle line:90%
sometimes you want to do that.

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:57.253 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes you don't.

00:21:57.253 --> 00:22:01.320 align:middle line:90%


00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:03.320 align:middle line:84%
Very often, whether
you want to do that

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:10.160 align:middle line:84%
or not depends on how exactly
are you going to use your PC

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:14.335 align:middle line:90%
factors and your dimensions.

00:22:14.335 --> 00:22:16.210 align:middle line:84%
Generally, I would say
that most of the time,

00:22:16.210 --> 00:22:19.970 align:middle line:84%
you want to normalize
it in a certain--

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:21.610 align:middle line:84%
you want to
normalize it in a way

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:24.570 align:middle line:84%
that you want the expected--
at least the expected variance

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:26.490 align:middle line:84%
along different--
along your dimensions

00:22:26.490 --> 00:22:27.900 align:middle line:90%
to be unit, to be similar.

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:30.410 align:middle line:90%


00:22:30.410 --> 00:22:32.890 align:middle line:84%
Then you're going to compute
your covariance matrix

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:36.490 align:middle line:90%
and calculate the eigenvalues.

00:22:36.490 --> 00:22:40.770 align:middle line:84%
And then you're going to order
them by decreasing eigenvalue.

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:44.990 align:middle line:84%
And then you're going to have a
certain cutoff, where you say,

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:46.570 align:middle line:90%
only the top--

00:22:46.570 --> 00:22:49.850 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to use only the
top x factors to model

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:52.010 align:middle line:90%
the of my data set.

00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:53.276 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:22:53.276 --> 00:22:55.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you said maybe
you would normalize it.

00:22:55.990 --> 00:22:59.450 align:middle line:84%
What would be a concrete example
of where it would be better

00:22:59.450 --> 00:23:00.750 align:middle line:90%
to not normalize it?

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:05.850 align:middle line:90%


00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:09.160 align:middle line:84%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Well,
here's an example.

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:12.130 align:middle line:90%


00:23:12.130 --> 00:23:15.190 align:middle line:84%
So actually-- yes, I
will start with this.

00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:19.825 align:middle line:84%
So this is an example of-- it's
a little bit of a toy example.

00:23:19.825 --> 00:23:21.450 align:middle line:84%
And I'll talk about
a real example too.

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:26.010 align:middle line:84%
So in this toy example, there's
the blue and the red data set.

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:31.610 align:middle line:84%
And the red one looks
like it's one-dimensional.

00:23:31.610 --> 00:23:35.390 align:middle line:84%
And the blue looks
like it's just a blob.

00:23:35.390 --> 00:23:37.410 align:middle line:84%
There's no direct
dimensionality.

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:40.630 align:middle line:84%
So if I tell you,
should we use PCA?

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:41.690 align:middle line:90%
Is there a way--

00:23:41.690 --> 00:23:44.130 align:middle line:84%
is PCA going to work
on the blue data set?

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:46.570 align:middle line:84%
Is it going to reduce
dimensionality?

00:23:46.570 --> 00:23:48.490 align:middle line:84%
And the answer would
be no, because there

00:23:48.490 --> 00:23:50.850 align:middle line:90%
is no dominant direction.

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:53.030 align:middle line:84%
And in the red one,
it definitely will.

00:23:53.030 --> 00:23:55.290 align:middle line:84%
It will pick the
right direction.

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:57.170 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's fun about
these two examples

00:23:57.170 --> 00:24:03.170 align:middle line:84%
is that these two are the same
data, except in the blue data

00:24:03.170 --> 00:24:08.610 align:middle line:84%
set, I've normalized the
variance along x and y.

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:11.770 align:middle line:90%
And in the red one, I haven't.

00:24:11.770 --> 00:24:24.190 align:middle line:84%
So if these two dimensions
were temperature versus--

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:26.740 align:middle line:90%


00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:30.820 align:middle line:84%
temperature in Seattle
versus pressure in Atlanta

00:24:30.820 --> 00:24:35.580 align:middle line:84%
or something, that are very
distinct and generally different

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:38.300 align:middle line:84%
things, where you're only
interested in correlation

00:24:38.300 --> 00:24:42.420 align:middle line:84%
and the relative variance
is not very meaningful,

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:43.980 align:middle line:84%
then you would want
to normalize it.

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:48.500 align:middle line:84%
Because otherwise, the units
are not going to be-- you

00:24:48.500 --> 00:24:50.740 align:middle line:84%
want to have somewhat
related units.

00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:53.880 align:middle line:84%
So the units will be standard
deviations if you normalize it.

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:56.320 align:middle line:84%
If you don't, the units
will be arbitrary.

00:24:56.320 --> 00:25:00.100 align:middle line:84%
And they don't-- it
wouldn't really make sense.

00:25:00.100 --> 00:25:06.100 align:middle line:90%
On the other hand, if this was--

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:08.420 align:middle line:84%
if x-axis was your
grades on quiz 1

00:25:08.420 --> 00:25:11.950 align:middle line:84%
and y-axis was your
grades on quiz 2,

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:14.830 align:middle line:84%
these two are the
same units now.

00:25:14.830 --> 00:25:17.250 align:middle line:84%
And you might not
want to normalize.

00:25:17.250 --> 00:25:19.270 align:middle line:90%
You want to actually--

00:25:19.270 --> 00:25:21.532 align:middle line:84%
these grades are-- these
numbers are meaningful

00:25:21.532 --> 00:25:22.490 align:middle line:90%
relative to each other.

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:24.430 align:middle line:84%
So there's not
necessarily-- you want

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:28.710 align:middle line:84%
to actually model the actual
grades, including the variance

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:29.890 align:middle line:90%
of quiz 1 and quiz 2.

00:25:29.890 --> 00:25:32.325 align:middle line:84%
So there is no necessarily
need to normalize it.

00:25:32.325 --> 00:25:33.450 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That makes sense.

00:25:33.450 --> 00:25:34.030 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:37.570 align:middle line:84%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Yeah, but
that part is-- to be honest,

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:40.670 align:middle line:84%
that part is very much
the art of things.

00:25:40.670 --> 00:25:42.210 align:middle line:84%
We have a lot of
discussion at work.

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:43.130 align:middle line:90%
Are we normalizing it?

00:25:43.130 --> 00:25:44.670 align:middle line:90%
How are we normalizing it?

00:25:44.670 --> 00:25:47.150 align:middle line:84%
What are we using
to normalize it?

00:25:47.150 --> 00:25:50.672 align:middle line:84%
That's like-- surprisingly,
this is-- sometimes

00:25:50.672 --> 00:25:52.630 align:middle line:84%
cutting-edge research is
to actually understand

00:25:52.630 --> 00:25:54.790 align:middle line:90%
how you normalize it.

00:25:54.790 --> 00:25:57.510 align:middle line:84%
These are the things that
actually make a difference.

00:25:57.510 --> 00:25:59.550 align:middle line:84%
The other thing I
mentioned is that we

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:01.390 align:middle line:90%
need to demean the data set.

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:03.390 align:middle line:90%
Why is that important?

00:26:03.390 --> 00:26:06.070 align:middle line:84%
Because remember, the
principal component analysis

00:26:06.070 --> 00:26:07.870 align:middle line:90%
is a rotation.

00:26:07.870 --> 00:26:12.070 align:middle line:84%
So it always rotates
around (0, 0).

00:26:12.070 --> 00:26:16.110 align:middle line:90%
So what 0 is, it matters a lot.

00:26:16.110 --> 00:26:18.867 align:middle line:84%
And this is two examples
of the same data set.

00:26:18.867 --> 00:26:20.450 align:middle line:84%
These two plots are
the same data set.

00:26:20.450 --> 00:26:23.510 align:middle line:84%
But one is not demeaned,
and the other is demeaned.

00:26:23.510 --> 00:26:26.030 align:middle line:84%
So this one here
is centered at 0.

00:26:26.030 --> 00:26:30.850 align:middle line:84%
And PCA finds that there is a
direction, a dominant direction.

00:26:30.850 --> 00:26:31.410 align:middle line:90%
It finds it.

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:33.390 align:middle line:90%
It works like it's supposed to.

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:35.830 align:middle line:84%
This one here is
not centered at 0.

00:26:35.830 --> 00:26:38.230 align:middle line:84%
For example, let's say you
do a bunch of stock prices.

00:26:38.230 --> 00:26:41.190 align:middle line:90%
You just put prices into PCA.

00:26:41.190 --> 00:26:42.890 align:middle line:84%
Stock prices are
not centered at 0.

00:26:42.890 --> 00:26:44.090 align:middle line:90%
They're always positive.

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:47.330 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to
have some direction,

00:26:47.330 --> 00:26:49.330 align:middle line:84%
but it's going to be
somewhere far away from 0.

00:26:49.330 --> 00:26:51.080 align:middle line:84%
When you run PCA on
it, it's going to be--

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:55.070 align:middle line:84%
it's going to try to rotate
around 0, but it's not.

00:26:55.070 --> 00:26:59.950 align:middle line:84%
It can't find anything because
it's not-- you're not--

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:01.110 align:middle line:90%
you can see why.

00:27:01.110 --> 00:27:02.530 align:middle line:90%
You can't rotate this thing.

00:27:02.530 --> 00:27:03.170 align:middle line:90%
You can't.

00:27:03.170 --> 00:27:06.680 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing to find if
you don't center it at 0.

00:27:06.680 --> 00:27:08.220 align:middle line:90%
You cannot rotate around.

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:11.680 align:middle line:84%
It's rotating always
around the origin.

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:18.580 align:middle line:84%
So mean near 0 or close to 0
or exactly 0 is very important.

00:27:18.580 --> 00:27:22.760 align:middle line:90%


00:27:22.760 --> 00:27:24.543 align:middle line:84%
And surprisingly,
it's surprising

00:27:24.543 --> 00:27:25.460 align:middle line:90%
how often it comes up.

00:27:25.460 --> 00:27:29.440 align:middle line:84%
Somebody just does PCA
on some numbers that are

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:32.840 align:middle line:90%
just large, positive numbers.

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:34.400 align:middle line:90%
And nothing comes out.

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:35.860 align:middle line:84%
And they're like,
it doesn't work.

00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:38.680 align:middle line:84%
But you just have
to normalize to 0.

00:27:38.680 --> 00:27:39.860 align:middle line:90%
How do we actually do it?

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:43.920 align:middle line:84%
Well, this is just the
examples of various places

00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:47.340 align:middle line:84%
that have implementations of
these eigenvalue calculations.

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:52.920 align:middle line:84%
I think when I started
doing this 11--

00:27:52.920 --> 00:27:56.520 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how many years ago,
this actually was more relevant.

00:27:56.520 --> 00:27:58.563 align:middle line:84%
Now, it's like
everybody uses Python.

00:27:58.563 --> 00:27:59.980 align:middle line:84%
Everybody knows
Jupyter Notebooks.

00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:03.970 align:middle line:84%
And you guys-- now
it's standard toolkit.

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:07.210 align:middle line:90%


00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:12.060 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's special about
finance when it comes to PCA?

00:28:12.060 --> 00:28:15.210 align:middle line:90%


00:28:15.210 --> 00:28:17.487 align:middle line:84%
Mostly, what is
special about finance--

00:28:17.487 --> 00:28:19.070 align:middle line:84%
and this is not
exclusive, by the way.

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:22.210 align:middle line:84%
But most of the
time, we are going

00:28:22.210 --> 00:28:26.130 align:middle line:90%
to be doing PCA on time series.

00:28:26.130 --> 00:28:32.390 align:middle line:84%
In quantitative finance, in
the Quant world that I live in

00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:38.810 align:middle line:84%
and I work in, I would
say, the general job

00:28:38.810 --> 00:28:44.370 align:middle line:84%
is to discover patterns that
have happened in the past

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:48.210 align:middle line:84%
and try to use that
information to model the future

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:52.690 align:middle line:84%
and to somehow use that
as part of your toolkit

00:28:52.690 --> 00:28:56.020 align:middle line:84%
to make predictions and to
place bets in the markets.

00:28:56.020 --> 00:28:58.730 align:middle line:90%


00:28:58.730 --> 00:29:04.700 align:middle line:84%
So every day, every trading
session, every observation

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:10.460 align:middle line:84%
is an opportunity to bet
and to make predictions.

00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:13.420 align:middle line:84%
And you can understand the
thing about time series

00:29:13.420 --> 00:29:16.700 align:middle line:84%
is that the history
now and the history

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:20.460 align:middle line:84%
on the next opportunities to
bet is not that different.

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:22.500 align:middle line:90%
It has one extra data point.

00:29:22.500 --> 00:29:24.560 align:middle line:84%
Later, it has an
additional data point,

00:29:24.560 --> 00:29:26.040 align:middle line:90%
but everything else is the same.

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:32.180 align:middle line:84%
So there is a slow-changing
time series of information.

00:29:32.180 --> 00:29:34.040 align:middle line:90%
And how we handle that--

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:38.020 align:middle line:84%
there is a whole time
series data science

00:29:38.020 --> 00:29:39.940 align:middle line:90%
class that one can take.

00:29:39.940 --> 00:29:44.540 align:middle line:84%
But especially
with regard to PCA,

00:29:44.540 --> 00:29:50.420 align:middle line:84%
it's important to
understand what

00:29:50.420 --> 00:29:53.150 align:middle line:84%
impact does that time
series aspect have on PCA.

00:29:53.150 --> 00:29:56.180 align:middle line:90%


00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:59.380 align:middle line:84%
A couple of things, I
think, are important.

00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:02.340 align:middle line:90%


00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:06.620 align:middle line:84%
We generally are going to
be interested in modeling

00:30:06.620 --> 00:30:11.100 align:middle line:84%
the changes of things, of
variables, not the levels.

00:30:11.100 --> 00:30:14.720 align:middle line:84%
Part of that is the thing
I was just talking about,

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:17.060 align:middle line:90%
which is demeaning.

00:30:17.060 --> 00:30:22.100 align:middle line:84%
And part of it is that when
you make bets in the market,

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:24.700 align:middle line:84%
you make money based
on how things change

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:27.860 align:middle line:84%
after your bet, not the
price, the absolute level

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:29.240 align:middle line:90%
of where the price is at.

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:33.438 align:middle line:84%
So returns are what matters,
not the price of something,

00:30:33.438 --> 00:30:34.230 align:middle line:90%
the absolute price.

00:30:34.230 --> 00:30:37.500 align:middle line:90%


00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:40.600 align:middle line:84%
Then the next thing
is, so when we do--

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:45.100 align:middle line:84%
when we model-- when we have
our data in finance, generally,

00:30:45.100 --> 00:30:49.120 align:middle line:84%
the data is the historical
returns or history of something.

00:30:49.120 --> 00:30:49.880 align:middle line:90%
History of prices.

00:30:49.880 --> 00:30:50.672 align:middle line:90%
History of returns.

00:30:50.672 --> 00:30:54.113 align:middle line:84%
History of anything, of
whatever you're modeling.

00:30:54.113 --> 00:30:55.530 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't have
to be just prices.

00:30:55.530 --> 00:30:58.830 align:middle line:90%
It could be anything that--

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:02.070 align:middle line:90%
any technical indicator.

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:05.030 align:middle line:84%
And that means that
every day, there

00:31:05.030 --> 00:31:07.610 align:middle line:84%
is a history that is similar,
but a little different.

00:31:07.610 --> 00:31:09.810 align:middle line:90%
So the history changes.

00:31:09.810 --> 00:31:14.550 align:middle line:84%
Your information about what
has happened changes slowly.

00:31:14.550 --> 00:31:19.230 align:middle line:84%
And that means that when
you're looking at your PCA,

00:31:19.230 --> 00:31:23.150 align:middle line:84%
you have to now
define, how am I going

00:31:23.150 --> 00:31:24.830 align:middle line:84%
to define my data
matrix, which I'm

00:31:24.830 --> 00:31:28.030 align:middle line:84%
using to calculate my
eigenvectors and eigenvalues?

00:31:28.030 --> 00:31:32.030 align:middle line:84%
And one of the main choices
are how long of a history

00:31:32.030 --> 00:31:35.630 align:middle line:90%
I want to use and how much--

00:31:35.630 --> 00:31:40.430 align:middle line:84%
how do I weight history,
more recent history

00:31:40.430 --> 00:31:43.830 align:middle line:90%
versus further away history?

00:31:43.830 --> 00:31:46.910 align:middle line:84%
And these are some of
the main hyperparameters

00:31:46.910 --> 00:31:50.470 align:middle line:84%
of principal component
analysis in finance.

00:31:50.470 --> 00:31:55.910 align:middle line:84%
How many things I'm
including in my data set.

00:31:55.910 --> 00:31:58.440 align:middle line:90%
How far back am I looking?

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:01.560 align:middle line:84%
How am I weighting
those observations

00:32:01.560 --> 00:32:04.800 align:middle line:90%
as I pass through time?

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:08.370 align:middle line:84%
And then the last thing is
the robustness of the data.

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:12.840 align:middle line:90%


00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:16.280 align:middle line:84%
Very often, there is a trade-off
between including more data

00:32:16.280 --> 00:32:18.560 align:middle line:90%
points.

00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:22.160 align:middle line:84%
But each marginal
addition of data--

00:32:22.160 --> 00:32:24.560 align:middle line:84%
each marginal dimension of
data that I'm looking at

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:28.580 align:middle line:90%
could be less robust.

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:32.580 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk about in
the examples later on.

00:32:32.580 --> 00:32:34.800 align:middle line:90%
But there are some--

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:40.920 align:middle line:84%
we have to think carefully
about how robust the data we're

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:41.540 align:middle line:90%
putting in is.

00:32:41.540 --> 00:32:46.240 align:middle line:84%
Because if it's too noisy, if
it's not representative of where

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:48.520 align:middle line:84%
our bets are going
to be, then it

00:32:48.520 --> 00:32:50.980 align:middle line:84%
becomes less relevant to
our modeling down the line.

00:32:50.980 --> 00:32:53.560 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, if
we don't include enough,

00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:56.010 align:middle line:84%
we're also going to be
missing opportunities

00:32:56.010 --> 00:32:59.670 align:middle line:84%
to understand the structure
of the information.

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:03.930 align:middle line:84%
So again, that's
part of the art of--

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:06.730 align:middle line:84%
actually, of the
practitioner, is to actually

00:33:06.730 --> 00:33:09.490 align:middle line:84%
have some feeling, have
some intuition about what

00:33:09.490 --> 00:33:11.770 align:middle line:84%
I should include and what
I should not include.

00:33:11.770 --> 00:33:14.850 align:middle line:84%
And we'll talk about
an example here.

00:33:14.850 --> 00:33:19.810 align:middle line:84%
The first example, and the main
example I want to go through,

00:33:19.810 --> 00:33:25.050 align:middle line:84%
is applying principal component
analysis to the United States

00:33:25.050 --> 00:33:25.830 align:middle line:90%
bond market.

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:26.628 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:33:26.628 --> 00:33:28.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well,
earlier you mentioned

00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:30.370 align:middle line:84%
that your time
[INAUDIBLE], in this case,

00:33:30.370 --> 00:33:36.050 align:middle line:84%
this time series was really,
like, historical [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:36.050 --> 00:33:40.930 align:middle line:84%
Is there any play with the
granularity of the time

00:33:40.930 --> 00:33:46.110 align:middle line:84%
series, the different,
I guess, window sizes,

00:33:46.110 --> 00:33:50.150 align:middle line:84%
I would say, of how granular
the data is going to be,

00:33:50.150 --> 00:33:52.090 align:middle line:90%
whether it's second--

00:33:52.090 --> 00:33:54.930 align:middle line:84%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Yeah, that's
a whole other dimension

00:33:54.930 --> 00:33:57.490 align:middle line:84%
of how granular it
is, absolutely right.

00:33:57.490 --> 00:34:00.050 align:middle line:84%
It's not just about
how long of a history,

00:34:00.050 --> 00:34:01.410 align:middle line:90%
but how granular it is.

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:04.010 align:middle line:90%


00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:08.130 align:middle line:84%
Well, the examples I'm
going to show you right now

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:12.449 align:middle line:90%
are based on daily observations.

00:34:12.449 --> 00:34:19.090 align:middle line:84%
But why did I decide to
use daily observations?

00:34:19.090 --> 00:34:24.170 align:middle line:84%
The short answer is every-- by
the way, very often, in my work,

00:34:24.170 --> 00:34:26.290 align:middle line:90%
we use daily observations.

00:34:26.290 --> 00:34:30.530 align:middle line:84%
The reason is that daily
observations are just

00:34:30.530 --> 00:34:31.469 align:middle line:90%
more available.

00:34:31.469 --> 00:34:34.449 align:middle line:84%
Generally, more
high-quality data history

00:34:34.449 --> 00:34:38.330 align:middle line:84%
is available for longer periods
of time for daily observations.

00:34:38.330 --> 00:34:43.889 align:middle line:84%
Second, yes, intraday
observations are--

00:34:43.889 --> 00:34:47.050 align:middle line:90%
they represent additional data.

00:34:47.050 --> 00:34:51.179 align:middle line:84%
However, the quality
of the imagery

00:34:51.179 --> 00:34:54.500 align:middle line:84%
that data, meaning how
much noise there is,

00:34:54.500 --> 00:34:57.980 align:middle line:84%
can very often, in practice, be
much higher for intraday data

00:34:57.980 --> 00:34:59.560 align:middle line:90%
than it is for end of day data.

00:34:59.560 --> 00:35:01.060 align:middle line:90%
That may not always be true.

00:35:01.060 --> 00:35:03.780 align:middle line:84%
There are situations
where that's not true.

00:35:03.780 --> 00:35:06.540 align:middle line:84%
And there's entire
fields in finance

00:35:06.540 --> 00:35:10.580 align:middle line:84%
that deal with high-frequency
trading in those fields

00:35:10.580 --> 00:35:14.240 align:middle line:84%
end of day data is just
like their whole trading,

00:35:14.240 --> 00:35:16.900 align:middle line:84%
buying, holding, and
selling happens 10 times

00:35:16.900 --> 00:35:17.880 align:middle line:90%
for the entire day.

00:35:17.880 --> 00:35:20.800 align:middle line:84%
So day start and end of
day with no positions.

00:35:20.800 --> 00:35:23.080 align:middle line:84%
And for them, daily
is not relevant.

00:35:23.080 --> 00:35:24.620 align:middle line:90%
It's too slow.

00:35:24.620 --> 00:35:26.860 align:middle line:84%
They need to understand
how the market moves

00:35:26.860 --> 00:35:31.020 align:middle line:90%
in a very fast timescale.

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:38.240 align:middle line:84%
So how often how you sample
time is a whole other story.

00:35:38.240 --> 00:35:40.220 align:middle line:84%
And basically,
how we sample time

00:35:40.220 --> 00:35:44.820 align:middle line:84%
is going to impact the units of
volatility because volatility

00:35:44.820 --> 00:35:49.130 align:middle line:84%
is measured in units of square
root of time, basically.

00:35:49.130 --> 00:35:55.110 align:middle line:84%
So it's very important to know
that and to understand, to keep

00:35:55.110 --> 00:35:58.170 align:middle line:84%
that in mind, that whatever
the frequency of sampling is,

00:35:58.170 --> 00:35:59.670 align:middle line:84%
that's going to
give you effectively

00:35:59.670 --> 00:36:01.790 align:middle line:90%
the unit of your volatility.

00:36:01.790 --> 00:36:03.350 align:middle line:84%
And then if you want
to annualize it,

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:05.690 align:middle line:84%
you need to apply
appropriate factors.

00:36:05.690 --> 00:36:08.190 align:middle line:84%
And comparing volatilities
of different units

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:09.730 align:middle line:90%
is not going to be meaningful.

00:36:09.730 --> 00:36:13.150 align:middle line:90%
So that's important.

00:36:13.150 --> 00:36:19.350 align:middle line:84%
Going back to this example,
the US bond market--

00:36:19.350 --> 00:36:21.510 align:middle line:90%
so why am I--

00:36:21.510 --> 00:36:23.350 align:middle line:84%
why is this an
interesting example?

00:36:23.350 --> 00:36:25.870 align:middle line:84%
I work in fixed income,
which is basically

00:36:25.870 --> 00:36:32.970 align:middle line:84%
where we trade bonds and
various derivatives of bonds.

00:36:32.970 --> 00:36:36.427 align:middle line:84%
And those are the biggest
markets in the world.

00:36:36.427 --> 00:36:38.510 align:middle line:84%
There's a huge amounts of
money being transferred.

00:36:38.510 --> 00:36:40.260 align:middle line:84%
They're not necessarily
the most volatile.

00:36:40.260 --> 00:36:41.930 align:middle line:90%
Equities are much more volatile.

00:36:41.930 --> 00:36:46.170 align:middle line:84%
But they are extremely
deep, extremely liquid.

00:36:46.170 --> 00:36:51.710 align:middle line:84%
And they also allow people
who trade in them to take

00:36:51.710 --> 00:36:53.190 align:middle line:90%
large amounts of leverage.

00:36:53.190 --> 00:36:55.950 align:middle line:84%
So even though they're
not by themselves

00:36:55.950 --> 00:36:58.690 align:middle line:84%
that volatile, if once
you take leverage,

00:36:58.690 --> 00:37:02.030 align:middle line:84%
you can increase volatility
by taking leverage.

00:37:02.030 --> 00:37:04.590 align:middle line:90%
So you can actually make--

00:37:04.590 --> 00:37:08.510 align:middle line:84%
you can construct portfolios
that are actually quite volatile

00:37:08.510 --> 00:37:16.390 align:middle line:84%
and deliver substantial
market volatility and returns.

00:37:16.390 --> 00:37:19.830 align:middle line:84%
So there's a lot
of huge industry

00:37:19.830 --> 00:37:24.030 align:middle line:84%
of money managers, hedge
funds that trade fixed income.

00:37:24.030 --> 00:37:28.190 align:middle line:84%
And the US bond market is
the prototypical example

00:37:28.190 --> 00:37:28.970 align:middle line:90%
of fixed income.

00:37:28.970 --> 00:37:30.345 align:middle line:84%
It's the most
liquid in the world

00:37:30.345 --> 00:37:33.310 align:middle line:84%
by far, the most
well-structured.

00:37:33.310 --> 00:37:37.590 align:middle line:84%
So it's a great
example to play with.

00:37:37.590 --> 00:37:41.230 align:middle line:84%
Another reason I'm using
it is because there

00:37:41.230 --> 00:37:44.270 align:middle line:84%
is pricing data from
the Federal Reserve

00:37:44.270 --> 00:37:46.360 align:middle line:90%
that is freely available.

00:37:46.360 --> 00:37:48.240 align:middle line:84%
And that's the data
that we're going

00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:51.920 align:middle line:84%
to use right now
to do our analysis,

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:54.710 align:middle line:84%
this data is like, it's actually
quite high-quality data.

00:37:54.710 --> 00:38:00.040 align:middle line:90%


00:38:00.040 --> 00:38:04.400 align:middle line:84%
I think that if you want
to do a project like this,

00:38:04.400 --> 00:38:08.960 align:middle line:84%
would be a great data set to
play with because it's real

00:38:08.960 --> 00:38:14.760 align:middle line:84%
and it goes on for
a very long time,

00:38:14.760 --> 00:38:18.880 align:middle line:84%
and you can apply you can
apply our PCA techniques to it.

00:38:18.880 --> 00:38:21.360 align:middle line:84%
So the first
question to ask is--

00:38:21.360 --> 00:38:24.080 align:middle line:84%
remember, we have
our data set now.

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:25.300 align:middle line:90%
What does it consist of?

00:38:25.300 --> 00:38:30.040 align:middle line:84%
Well, in the United
States, the US Treasury

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:34.080 align:middle line:84%
issues a bunch of T-bills,
short maturity bills,

00:38:34.080 --> 00:38:35.700 align:middle line:90%
that they raise money.

00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:38.560 align:middle line:84%
That's how they raise
money for the debt.

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:40.640 align:middle line:84%
But the part we're
going to concern with

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:44.130 align:middle line:84%
is going to be the US
Treasury notes and bonds which

00:38:44.130 --> 00:38:46.890 align:middle line:84%
are kind of the
longer-dated instruments

00:38:46.890 --> 00:38:48.490 align:middle line:90%
that US Treasury issues.

00:38:48.490 --> 00:38:50.610 align:middle line:84%
They issue on a
very regular basis--

00:38:50.610 --> 00:38:54.530 align:middle line:84%
every month, there is a
schedule of auctions that is

00:38:54.530 --> 00:38:57.970 align:middle line:90%
very well known and repetitive.

00:38:57.970 --> 00:39:00.790 align:middle line:84%
And they always issue a
2-year bond, a 3-year bond,

00:39:00.790 --> 00:39:02.830 align:middle line:84%
5-year bond, 7-year
bond, 10-year bond,

00:39:02.830 --> 00:39:04.770 align:middle line:90%
and a 30-year bond.

00:39:04.770 --> 00:39:08.330 align:middle line:84%
And there's actually now a
20-year bond that started about

00:39:08.330 --> 00:39:09.730 align:middle line:90%
three or four years ago.

00:39:09.730 --> 00:39:14.370 align:middle line:84%
But we're not going to look at
that right now because it's very

00:39:14.370 --> 00:39:16.070 align:middle line:90%
new and it's not as liquid.

00:39:16.070 --> 00:39:19.610 align:middle line:84%
So we're just going
to focus on these.

00:39:19.610 --> 00:39:22.570 align:middle line:84%
And the other thing
about these bonds

00:39:22.570 --> 00:39:26.590 align:middle line:84%
is that they're all
debts of the US Treasury.

00:39:26.590 --> 00:39:29.570 align:middle line:84%
So in a sense,
they're very similar.

00:39:29.570 --> 00:39:33.150 align:middle line:84%
If I give you money
for six months

00:39:33.150 --> 00:39:37.610 align:middle line:84%
and I also give you a
separate loan for three years,

00:39:37.610 --> 00:39:38.710 align:middle line:90%
I've given you two loans.

00:39:38.710 --> 00:39:41.000 align:middle line:84%
The first thing that
these two things are--

00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:43.972 align:middle line:84%
basically, you
still owe me money.

00:39:43.972 --> 00:39:45.680 align:middle line:84%
One is longer in
maturity than the other,

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:47.960 align:middle line:90%
but you're still owing me money.

00:39:47.960 --> 00:39:53.220 align:middle line:84%
So in terms of the way the
price of these securities

00:39:53.220 --> 00:39:56.820 align:middle line:84%
evolves, they all
depend on the interest

00:39:56.820 --> 00:40:00.860 align:middle line:84%
rates, the path of short-term
interest rates of the Federal

00:40:00.860 --> 00:40:03.560 align:middle line:84%
Reserve, and the
expectations of that path.

00:40:03.560 --> 00:40:07.300 align:middle line:84%
So they're all
derivatives of that.

00:40:07.300 --> 00:40:11.340 align:middle line:84%
The implications of
that is that the returns

00:40:11.340 --> 00:40:17.640 align:middle line:84%
or the changes of bond yields
are very highly correlated.

00:40:17.640 --> 00:40:19.100 align:middle line:84%
So here, on the
bottom right, I've

00:40:19.100 --> 00:40:24.642 align:middle line:84%
shown the correlation
matrix of changes.

00:40:24.642 --> 00:40:27.100 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that it's like
these numbers are very, very

00:40:27.100 --> 00:40:28.820 align:middle line:90%
high.

00:40:28.820 --> 00:40:32.860 align:middle line:84%
So this is a data
set where everything

00:40:32.860 --> 00:40:38.140 align:middle line:84%
is very, very highly correlated
prime candidate for PCA.

00:40:38.140 --> 00:40:39.800 align:middle line:84%
Whenever you see a
correlation matrix,

00:40:39.800 --> 00:40:45.020 align:middle line:84%
it has high concentrations
of high correlations,

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:46.900 align:middle line:90%
the prime candidate of PCA.

00:40:46.900 --> 00:40:49.140 align:middle line:84%
So what are these
numbers I'm looking at?

00:40:49.140 --> 00:40:53.020 align:middle line:84%
On the left chart, I've
shown the US yield curve.

00:40:53.020 --> 00:40:58.300 align:middle line:84%
So on any given day, we can
observe the prices of 2-year

00:40:58.300 --> 00:41:00.580 align:middle line:84%
bonds, 3-year bonds,
5-year bonds, 7-year bonds,

00:41:00.580 --> 00:41:02.180 align:middle line:90%
10-year bonds, 30-year bonds.

00:41:02.180 --> 00:41:03.780 align:middle line:90%
What is the price of that?

00:41:03.780 --> 00:41:06.980 align:middle line:90%
And I don't know if--

00:41:06.980 --> 00:41:11.220 align:middle line:84%
Peter, have you guys
done bond math yet?

00:41:11.220 --> 00:41:13.338 align:middle line:84%
PETER: A little bit
bond math, a little.

00:41:13.338 --> 00:41:14.380 align:middle line:90%
STEFAN ANDREEV: A little.

00:41:14.380 --> 00:41:17.160 align:middle line:84%
So we know the prices where
these things buy and sell.

00:41:17.160 --> 00:41:18.680 align:middle line:90%
They're very transparent prices.

00:41:18.680 --> 00:41:22.220 align:middle line:84%
And we can convert
prices to yields.

00:41:22.220 --> 00:41:24.740 align:middle line:84%
So basically, a yield
is like the implied rate

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:27.140 align:middle line:84%
of return of the
bond, effectively,

00:41:27.140 --> 00:41:30.260 align:middle line:84%
given its price over the
course of its maturity.

00:41:30.260 --> 00:41:34.700 align:middle line:84%
And then, when we plot these
yields, on this plot here,

00:41:34.700 --> 00:41:36.990 align:middle line:90%
I've plotted the yields.

00:41:36.990 --> 00:41:40.050 align:middle line:84%
The yield is on the y-axis
versus the maturity of the bond.

00:41:40.050 --> 00:41:44.670 align:middle line:84%
And I've sampled five
days from history.

00:41:44.670 --> 00:41:47.550 align:middle line:84%
And this is what's known
as the yield curve.

00:41:47.550 --> 00:41:52.490 align:middle line:84%
That is the most important input
of macroeconomics in the world

00:41:52.490 --> 00:41:55.090 align:middle line:84%
is the US-- the shape of
the dollar yield curve.

00:41:55.090 --> 00:41:58.670 align:middle line:90%


00:41:58.670 --> 00:42:01.410 align:middle line:84%
Most of the time, the yield
curve is upward sloping,

00:42:01.410 --> 00:42:06.330 align:middle line:84%
so people require a bigger
return to lend money for longer.

00:42:06.330 --> 00:42:09.390 align:middle line:84%
And the US Treasury
generally requires that.

00:42:09.390 --> 00:42:11.030 align:middle line:90%
But sometimes, that is not true.

00:42:11.030 --> 00:42:12.890 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, you can see
this top blue line.

00:42:12.890 --> 00:42:16.110 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, the yields actually--
it starts out downward sloping

00:42:16.110 --> 00:42:17.590 align:middle line:90%
and then flattens out.

00:42:17.590 --> 00:42:20.950 align:middle line:84%
So these yields can have
a variety of shapes.

00:42:20.950 --> 00:42:24.028 align:middle line:84%
And this is just
the level of yields.

00:42:24.028 --> 00:42:25.570 align:middle line:84%
What we're betting
on is the changes.

00:42:25.570 --> 00:42:29.390 align:middle line:84%
So we're betting on how this
curve evolves day to day.

00:42:29.390 --> 00:42:32.990 align:middle line:84%
And you can see from
these yield curves

00:42:32.990 --> 00:42:36.475 align:middle line:84%
that you can imagine
how, if I were

00:42:36.475 --> 00:42:38.100 align:middle line:84%
to show you a movie
of the yield curve,

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:39.900 align:middle line:90%
it's going to flop around.

00:42:39.900 --> 00:42:42.162 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be like little
strings that flop around.

00:42:42.162 --> 00:42:43.620 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be
going up and down.

00:42:43.620 --> 00:42:45.880 align:middle line:90%
It's going to slope, like this.

00:42:45.880 --> 00:42:48.060 align:middle line:84%
It's going to have a
whole set of dynamics.

00:42:48.060 --> 00:42:50.680 align:middle line:90%


00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:53.120 align:middle line:84%
And that those
dynamics is what we're

00:42:53.120 --> 00:42:55.420 align:middle line:90%
trying to model with PCA here.

00:42:55.420 --> 00:43:00.040 align:middle line:90%


00:43:00.040 --> 00:43:04.400 align:middle line:84%
Bonus question is
do you guys know

00:43:04.400 --> 00:43:06.880 align:middle line:84%
what is the shape of
the yield curve now?

00:43:06.880 --> 00:43:09.320 align:middle line:90%
What does it look like?

00:43:09.320 --> 00:43:12.310 align:middle line:84%
Are short-term interest rates
higher than long-term interest

00:43:12.310 --> 00:43:12.810 align:middle line:90%
rates?

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:16.200 align:middle line:90%


00:43:16.200 --> 00:43:22.480 align:middle line:84%
Has anybody-- we had a very
prominent Federal Reserve

00:43:22.480 --> 00:43:29.800 align:middle line:84%
meeting recently, and do
you remember what they did?

00:43:29.800 --> 00:43:31.998 align:middle line:90%
The action the Fed took was--

00:43:31.998 --> 00:43:34.040 align:middle line:84%
the action the Fed took
was to cut interest rates

00:43:34.040 --> 00:43:35.760 align:middle line:90%
by 50 basis points.

00:43:35.760 --> 00:43:37.542 align:middle line:90%
So what the Fed is doing--

00:43:37.542 --> 00:43:39.000 align:middle line:84%
it's cutting the
interest rate that

00:43:39.000 --> 00:43:40.880 align:middle line:84%
is the leftmost point
on this yield curve

00:43:40.880 --> 00:43:44.200 align:middle line:90%
that they're setting that point.

00:43:44.200 --> 00:43:46.240 align:middle line:84%
Everything else, the
rest of the curve,

00:43:46.240 --> 00:43:48.960 align:middle line:84%
is market expectations
of what that point will

00:43:48.960 --> 00:43:50.640 align:middle line:90%
do in the future.

00:43:50.640 --> 00:43:53.280 align:middle line:84%
So they moved this point
down by 50 basis points.

00:43:53.280 --> 00:43:55.720 align:middle line:84%
50 basis points
means half a percent.

00:43:55.720 --> 00:44:00.440 align:middle line:84%
So it went down
by half a percent.

00:44:00.440 --> 00:44:04.640 align:middle line:84%
Right now, this blue curve,
even though it's from 2023,

00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:07.560 align:middle line:90%
that's pretty recent.

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:09.580 align:middle line:84%
The curve is still
downward sloping.

00:44:09.580 --> 00:44:13.920 align:middle line:84%
It looks a little bit
more like this right now.

00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:16.600 align:middle line:84%
For a very long time, it was
actually really inverted.

00:44:16.600 --> 00:44:19.880 align:middle line:84%
It was going downward
sloping, like that.

00:44:19.880 --> 00:44:21.900 align:middle line:84%
Now the back is
starting to flatten out,

00:44:21.900 --> 00:44:25.890 align:middle line:84%
but it's still quite high in the
front end and downward sloping.

00:44:25.890 --> 00:44:30.320 align:middle line:90%


00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:32.950 align:middle line:84%
So unrelated to what
we're talking about,

00:44:32.950 --> 00:44:37.110 align:middle line:90%
but kind of a fun fact.

00:44:37.110 --> 00:44:45.090 align:middle line:84%
So now, when we look at your
changes, when we look at the--

00:44:45.090 --> 00:44:48.450 align:middle line:84%
we take our data, we compute
yield changes, so the changes

00:44:48.450 --> 00:44:51.890 align:middle line:90%
of yields on day to day.

00:44:51.890 --> 00:44:54.430 align:middle line:90%
We should demean that data set.

00:44:54.430 --> 00:44:56.210 align:middle line:84%
But generally,
demeaning doesn't really

00:44:56.210 --> 00:44:59.170 align:middle line:84%
help that much because the mean
is going to be very close to 0

00:44:59.170 --> 00:45:01.310 align:middle line:84%
because changes are
sometimes going to go up.

00:45:01.310 --> 00:45:05.010 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, they're
going to go down.

00:45:05.010 --> 00:45:07.108 align:middle line:84%
Over the history
of 20 or 30 years

00:45:07.108 --> 00:45:08.650 align:middle line:84%
that we're talking
about here, you're

00:45:08.650 --> 00:45:12.570 align:middle line:90%
not going to have a large mean.

00:45:12.570 --> 00:45:15.860 align:middle line:84%
Still, it's always good
practice to demean it.

00:45:15.860 --> 00:45:18.410 align:middle line:84%
It won't change,
much, the result.

00:45:18.410 --> 00:45:25.110 align:middle line:84%
But in this example, I didn't
actually normalize the variance.

00:45:25.110 --> 00:45:27.570 align:middle line:84%
I think we just
left it in there.

00:45:27.570 --> 00:45:29.760 align:middle line:84%
So we did PCA on
covariance matrix,

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:32.620 align:middle line:84%
and the reason is that the
units, the yield changes

00:45:32.620 --> 00:45:34.480 align:middle line:90%
are comparable units.

00:45:34.480 --> 00:45:40.660 align:middle line:84%
And looking at the PCA
without normalizing

00:45:40.660 --> 00:45:43.140 align:middle line:90%
the variance is actually--

00:45:43.140 --> 00:45:46.580 align:middle line:90%
it's a very meaningful output.

00:45:46.580 --> 00:45:49.620 align:middle line:84%
We want to understand
how volatile.

00:45:49.620 --> 00:45:52.340 align:middle line:84%
It's going to tell us something
about the relative volatilities

00:45:52.340 --> 00:45:55.100 align:middle line:90%
of points.

00:45:55.100 --> 00:45:59.185 align:middle line:84%
So when we do that
and then we look at--

00:45:59.185 --> 00:46:01.140 align:middle line:84%
what I'm plotting
here on the top

00:46:01.140 --> 00:46:06.798 align:middle line:84%
are the eigenvectors, so the
principal component factors,

00:46:06.798 --> 00:46:08.340 align:middle line:84%
the shapes of the
principal component

00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:11.460 align:middle line:90%
factors on various days.

00:46:11.460 --> 00:46:13.440 align:middle line:90%
So this plot can be confusing.

00:46:13.440 --> 00:46:15.500 align:middle line:84%
And I want to make
sure that we're all

00:46:15.500 --> 00:46:16.880 align:middle line:90%
on the same page what it is.

00:46:16.880 --> 00:46:20.080 align:middle line:90%


00:46:20.080 --> 00:46:22.758 align:middle line:90%
This is the yields.

00:46:22.758 --> 00:46:23.800 align:middle line:90%
And we take these yields.

00:46:23.800 --> 00:46:26.360 align:middle line:84%
We look at the
changes of yields.

00:46:26.360 --> 00:46:28.750 align:middle line:90%
And we put them through PCA.

00:46:28.750 --> 00:46:32.010 align:middle line:84%
And remember, the output of PCA
is eigenvectors and eigenvalues.

00:46:32.010 --> 00:46:36.570 align:middle line:84%
Eigenvectors means every point,
like 2-year, 3-year, 5-year,

00:46:36.570 --> 00:46:37.770 align:middle line:90%
10-year, 30-year.

00:46:37.770 --> 00:46:40.910 align:middle line:84%
Everything has a
number now, loading.

00:46:40.910 --> 00:46:45.030 align:middle line:84%
We call that, the number,
the eigenvector coefficient.

00:46:45.030 --> 00:46:46.050 align:middle line:90%
I call that loading.

00:46:46.050 --> 00:46:50.590 align:middle line:90%
That's a term that we use.

00:46:50.590 --> 00:46:54.370 align:middle line:84%
That loading is, again,
there's a time series of those.

00:46:54.370 --> 00:47:00.910 align:middle line:84%
And I'm plotting here select
five different dates in history

00:47:00.910 --> 00:47:03.590 align:middle line:84%
of what that eigenvector
looks like-- what

00:47:03.590 --> 00:47:05.790 align:middle line:84%
the first principal
component looks like,

00:47:05.790 --> 00:47:07.810 align:middle line:84%
what the second principal
component looks like,

00:47:07.810 --> 00:47:09.850 align:middle line:84%
what the third principal
component looks like.

00:47:09.850 --> 00:47:12.510 align:middle line:90%


00:47:12.510 --> 00:47:18.910 align:middle line:84%
These are snapshots, like
five snapshots in time.

00:47:18.910 --> 00:47:22.830 align:middle line:84%
And I'm plotting
maturity versus loading.

00:47:22.830 --> 00:47:24.890 align:middle line:84%
And there's actually
three dimensions here.

00:47:24.890 --> 00:47:26.450 align:middle line:84%
There's maturity,
loading, and time.

00:47:26.450 --> 00:47:29.110 align:middle line:84%
So I can show you a
three-dimensional plot, but that

00:47:29.110 --> 00:47:31.590 align:middle line:90%
would just be like confusing.

00:47:31.590 --> 00:47:33.530 align:middle line:84%
So I'm showing
here cross-section.

00:47:33.530 --> 00:47:37.670 align:middle line:84%
So these are various
cross-sections on time.

00:47:37.670 --> 00:47:43.590 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that there is
a clear and persistent pattern

00:47:43.590 --> 00:47:47.870 align:middle line:84%
in what the
eigenvectors look like.

00:47:47.870 --> 00:47:52.430 align:middle line:84%
The first one, PC1,
is called the level.

00:47:52.430 --> 00:47:53.956 align:middle line:90%
That basically means that--

00:47:53.956 --> 00:47:57.070 align:middle line:90%


00:47:57.070 --> 00:47:58.490 align:middle line:90%
all the loadings are positive.

00:47:58.490 --> 00:48:00.250 align:middle line:84%
So it means when the
yield curves move,

00:48:00.250 --> 00:48:05.050 align:middle line:84%
generally, they move up and down
together in the same direction.

00:48:05.050 --> 00:48:08.950 align:middle line:84%
So if 10-year rate is going up,
probably 2-year rate is going up

00:48:08.950 --> 00:48:11.430 align:middle line:84%
as well, 5-year rate
is going up as well.

00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:14.350 align:middle line:84%
And hence the term
rates are going up.

00:48:14.350 --> 00:48:16.358 align:middle line:84%
Rates are going up means,
generally speaking,

00:48:16.358 --> 00:48:17.650 align:middle line:90%
everything is going up or down.

00:48:17.650 --> 00:48:21.310 align:middle line:84%
And that is the
move that explains

00:48:21.310 --> 00:48:24.880 align:middle line:84%
most of the variance
of the yield curve.

00:48:24.880 --> 00:48:27.740 align:middle line:84%
But, on top of that, now
there is a second move,

00:48:27.740 --> 00:48:29.120 align:middle line:90%
which is we call this PC2.

00:48:29.120 --> 00:48:32.140 align:middle line:84%
And when we look at the
shape of it, we can say,

00:48:32.140 --> 00:48:35.760 align:middle line:84%
oh it starts out negative on
the front end of the curve

00:48:35.760 --> 00:48:39.020 align:middle line:84%
and it's positive on
the back end the curve.

00:48:39.020 --> 00:48:41.120 align:middle line:90%
That's the slope.

00:48:41.120 --> 00:48:44.000 align:middle line:84%
So that's kind of a steepening
of the curve versus flattening

00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:45.000 align:middle line:90%
of the curve.

00:48:45.000 --> 00:48:47.700 align:middle line:90%
That is the second PC factor.

00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:49.200 align:middle line:84%
So this first PC
factor, by the way,

00:48:49.200 --> 00:48:53.500 align:middle line:84%
explains around like 90% of the
variance, maybe 85 or something.

00:48:53.500 --> 00:48:57.200 align:middle line:84%
And the second PC factor
explains between 5% and 10%

00:48:57.200 --> 00:48:59.080 align:middle line:90%
of the variance--

00:48:59.080 --> 00:49:01.840 align:middle line:90%
very huge difference.

00:49:01.840 --> 00:49:06.520 align:middle line:84%
And the third factor is like
the curvature, so how curvy--

00:49:06.520 --> 00:49:08.600 align:middle line:90%
what is the curvature of the--

00:49:08.600 --> 00:49:11.760 align:middle line:84%
it's positive on the front end,
positive at the third year,

00:49:11.760 --> 00:49:17.040 align:middle line:84%
and negative at the belly
of the curve, 10-year point.

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:18.982 align:middle line:84%
And that has the smallest
amount of variance.

00:49:18.982 --> 00:49:19.940 align:middle line:90%
So what does that mean?

00:49:19.940 --> 00:49:22.482 align:middle line:84%
If you think mentally of the
movie of what the yield curve is

00:49:22.482 --> 00:49:24.572 align:middle line:84%
doing, it's mostly
going up and down,

00:49:24.572 --> 00:49:26.030 align:middle line:84%
mostly going up
and down like that.

00:49:26.030 --> 00:49:29.910 align:middle line:84%
But it's also slope,
a little bit of slope.

00:49:29.910 --> 00:49:32.370 align:middle line:84%
So it's mostly up and down,
but a little bit of slope,

00:49:32.370 --> 00:49:36.330 align:middle line:84%
and a little bit of
curvature, like that.

00:49:36.330 --> 00:49:40.790 align:middle line:84%
So that's what this thing tells
us is the dynamics of the curve.

00:49:40.790 --> 00:49:43.530 align:middle line:90%


00:49:43.530 --> 00:49:47.530 align:middle line:90%
Now some of these things are--

00:49:47.530 --> 00:49:50.130 align:middle line:84%
the structure of this thing
actually tells you a lot.

00:49:50.130 --> 00:50:00.770 align:middle line:84%
You can see how the PC1 level,
the maturity at which it--

00:50:00.770 --> 00:50:04.970 align:middle line:84%
it starts out steep, upward
sloping, and then it levels out.

00:50:04.970 --> 00:50:07.810 align:middle line:84%
The maturity in years
at which it levels out--

00:50:07.810 --> 00:50:10.090 align:middle line:84%
I've plotted here what
the maturity is, at which

00:50:10.090 --> 00:50:13.250 align:middle line:84%
it kind of levels out, where
the short-term rate decoupled

00:50:13.250 --> 00:50:14.730 align:middle line:90%
from long-term rates.

00:50:14.730 --> 00:50:18.110 align:middle line:84%
And if you plot
that in time series,

00:50:18.110 --> 00:50:25.530 align:middle line:84%
you can see that
it was very short.

00:50:25.530 --> 00:50:28.770 align:middle line:84%
Monetary policy was
basically only governing

00:50:28.770 --> 00:50:33.130 align:middle line:84%
the short end of the curve up
to two years up until right

00:50:33.130 --> 00:50:34.630 align:middle line:90%
after the post-financial crisis.

00:50:34.630 --> 00:50:37.330 align:middle line:84%
And then the Fed became
very, very powerful

00:50:37.330 --> 00:50:38.470 align:middle line:90%
post-financial crisis.

00:50:38.470 --> 00:50:40.450 align:middle line:84%
The Fed said,
we're going to keep

00:50:40.450 --> 00:50:42.165 align:middle line:90%
rates low for a very long time.

00:50:42.165 --> 00:50:44.290 align:middle line:84%
And all of a sudden, this
thing exploded and became

00:50:44.290 --> 00:50:46.430 align:middle line:90%
very, very, very high.

00:50:46.430 --> 00:50:51.610 align:middle line:84%
So basically, the short
end rates were governing,

00:50:51.610 --> 00:50:56.850 align:middle line:84%
effectively, the rates
up to four or five years.

00:50:56.850 --> 00:50:58.530 align:middle line:84%
And then, up to
COVID, they started

00:50:58.530 --> 00:51:00.342 align:middle line:90%
to decrease up until COVID.

00:51:00.342 --> 00:51:02.490 align:middle line:84%
COVID happened, Fed
came in and said,

00:51:02.490 --> 00:51:03.910 align:middle line:84%
we're going to
bring the bazooka.

00:51:03.910 --> 00:51:07.730 align:middle line:84%
So everybody's like, OK,
they're going to keep rates

00:51:07.730 --> 00:51:09.170 align:middle line:90%
low for a long time as well.

00:51:09.170 --> 00:51:13.010 align:middle line:84%
It exploded again, and
now it's back down again.

00:51:13.010 --> 00:51:16.530 align:middle line:84%
So that's already
something that is kind

00:51:16.530 --> 00:51:17.760 align:middle line:90%
of like a regime indicator.

00:51:17.760 --> 00:51:20.020 align:middle line:84%
You can see how
the market behavior

00:51:20.020 --> 00:51:23.203 align:middle line:84%
is reflected in the shape
of these PC factors.

00:51:23.203 --> 00:51:25.620 align:middle line:84%
That tells you so much about
what's going on in the market

00:51:25.620 --> 00:51:29.860 align:middle line:84%
when you look in the
details of these factors.

00:51:29.860 --> 00:51:34.260 align:middle line:84%
Something like where
is the bottom of PC3?

00:51:34.260 --> 00:51:35.860 align:middle line:90%
Where is the belly?

00:51:35.860 --> 00:51:38.160 align:middle line:84%
You can see that,
sometimes, it's at 10 years.

00:51:38.160 --> 00:51:39.760 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, it
becomes close to 5.

00:51:39.760 --> 00:51:41.660 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes, it's close to 15.

00:51:41.660 --> 00:51:45.340 align:middle line:90%
That's also a regime indicator.

00:51:45.340 --> 00:51:46.560 align:middle line:90%
PC loading stability.

00:51:46.560 --> 00:51:49.740 align:middle line:84%
So here I'm taking
this graph PC1 graph.

00:51:49.740 --> 00:51:53.100 align:middle line:84%
But instead of plotting
loading versus maturity,

00:51:53.100 --> 00:51:55.860 align:middle line:84%
I'm plotting
loading versus time.

00:51:55.860 --> 00:52:00.260 align:middle line:90%
And each maturity point is line.

00:52:00.260 --> 00:52:02.820 align:middle line:84%
So it tells me the
stability of my loadings.

00:52:02.820 --> 00:52:05.740 align:middle line:84%
Generally, you want to
look at that because you

00:52:05.740 --> 00:52:08.420 align:middle line:90%
want factors that are stable.

00:52:08.420 --> 00:52:10.960 align:middle line:84%
And PC1 loadings
are pretty stable.

00:52:10.960 --> 00:52:16.790 align:middle line:84%
But where the changes
in PC1 loadings,

00:52:16.790 --> 00:52:18.450 align:middle line:84%
very often, they're
quite meaningful.

00:52:18.450 --> 00:52:21.710 align:middle line:90%
In 2008 crisis, 2-year rate--

00:52:21.710 --> 00:52:23.750 align:middle line:90%
this blue line is a 2-year rate.

00:52:23.750 --> 00:52:25.250 align:middle line:84%
The short end becomes
very volatile.

00:52:25.250 --> 00:52:26.958 align:middle line:84%
Nobody knows what the
Fed is going to do.

00:52:26.958 --> 00:52:28.430 align:middle line:90%
There's a lot of volatility.

00:52:28.430 --> 00:52:31.390 align:middle line:90%
It then collapses post-2012.

00:52:31.390 --> 00:52:34.670 align:middle line:84%
The Fed is not doing anything,
so the 2-year point is not

00:52:34.670 --> 00:52:36.350 align:middle line:90%
moving.

00:52:36.350 --> 00:52:37.130 align:middle line:90%
Then it goes up.

00:52:37.130 --> 00:52:38.490 align:middle line:90%
Maybe the Fed is going to move.

00:52:38.490 --> 00:52:42.005 align:middle line:84%
Then, after COVID,
it also collapses.

00:52:42.005 --> 00:52:43.130 align:middle line:90%
And now it's back up again.

00:52:43.130 --> 00:52:44.670 align:middle line:84%
The Fed is very
active right now.

00:52:44.670 --> 00:52:48.015 align:middle line:84%
This ends in 2023,
this data set.

00:52:48.015 --> 00:52:49.390 align:middle line:84%
If I make it a
little bit longer,

00:52:49.390 --> 00:52:53.060 align:middle line:84%
you see this line actually went
very high in the recent history.

00:52:53.060 --> 00:52:56.270 align:middle line:90%


00:52:56.270 --> 00:52:59.030 align:middle line:90%
So these things are--

00:52:59.030 --> 00:53:05.230 align:middle line:84%
they all come from
this data, but they

00:53:05.230 --> 00:53:08.030 align:middle line:84%
tell you a lot about the
behavior, the regime,

00:53:08.030 --> 00:53:12.630 align:middle line:84%
and the dynamics of
interest rate market.

00:53:12.630 --> 00:53:14.520 align:middle line:90%
This is the variance explained.

00:53:14.520 --> 00:53:17.480 align:middle line:84%
I think this is a way--
remember how I was telling you

00:53:17.480 --> 00:53:20.440 align:middle line:84%
how we look at
eigenvalues and we

00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:24.020 align:middle line:84%
want to make sure that
they're well-segregated?

00:53:24.020 --> 00:53:27.560 align:middle line:84%
Well, this is a
way to look at it.

00:53:27.560 --> 00:53:29.680 align:middle line:84%
If you look at this chart,
and the first factor

00:53:29.680 --> 00:53:32.040 align:middle line:84%
explains so much
variance, it's like, yeah,

00:53:32.040 --> 00:53:34.380 align:middle line:90%
that's a good factor.

00:53:34.380 --> 00:53:35.520 align:middle line:90%
That's a good factor.

00:53:35.520 --> 00:53:37.360 align:middle line:90%
That's a solid factor.

00:53:37.360 --> 00:53:42.200 align:middle line:84%
And you know that this
thing is persistent.

00:53:42.200 --> 00:53:44.000 align:middle line:84%
This thing is
likely to continue.

00:53:44.000 --> 00:53:45.580 align:middle line:90%
It's likely to be stable.

00:53:45.580 --> 00:53:48.303 align:middle line:84%
And it's a good part of
your model for the future.

00:53:48.303 --> 00:53:50.220 align:middle line:84%
If you look at variance
explained by a factor,

00:53:50.220 --> 00:53:52.440 align:middle line:90%
this is a log plot.

00:53:52.440 --> 00:53:55.560 align:middle line:84%
You see how the first and
second and third factor

00:53:55.560 --> 00:53:59.320 align:middle line:84%
are separated by order of
magnitude, so they never cross?

00:53:59.320 --> 00:54:02.720 align:middle line:84%
So that means that
these factors are robust

00:54:02.720 --> 00:54:09.280 align:middle line:84%
and they're very useful
factors for modeling.

00:54:09.280 --> 00:54:11.180 align:middle line:84%
You want to see this
kind of picture.

00:54:11.180 --> 00:54:13.760 align:middle line:84%
What you don't want to see
is like lines that are all

00:54:13.760 --> 00:54:14.995 align:middle line:90%
tangled up with each other.

00:54:14.995 --> 00:54:16.120 align:middle line:90%
You don't want to see that.

00:54:16.120 --> 00:54:18.078 align:middle line:84%
You want to see something
like this in general,

00:54:18.078 --> 00:54:20.920 align:middle line:90%
when you're looking at PCA.

00:54:20.920 --> 00:54:24.140 align:middle line:84%
Again, these are eigenvalues I'm
looking at-- variance explained.

00:54:24.140 --> 00:54:29.200 align:middle line:90%


00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:32.200 align:middle line:84%
And this out-of-sample
covariance is when I'm--

00:54:32.200 --> 00:54:38.160 align:middle line:84%
this is basically when I
calibrate my PCA, remember,

00:54:38.160 --> 00:54:41.540 align:middle line:84%
if I take a single data
set, however I choose it,

00:54:41.540 --> 00:54:47.120 align:middle line:84%
and I do run PCA, the PC1
projections and the PC2

00:54:47.120 --> 00:54:50.720 align:middle line:84%
projections are going to be
uncorrelated with each other

00:54:50.720 --> 00:54:52.280 align:middle line:90%
by construction.

00:54:52.280 --> 00:54:54.600 align:middle line:84%
But if I take the
same loadings now

00:54:54.600 --> 00:54:59.240 align:middle line:84%
and I look at the
future returns,

00:54:59.240 --> 00:55:03.660 align:middle line:84%
there is nothing that says that
if I project my future data,

00:55:03.660 --> 00:55:05.640 align:middle line:84%
future returns onto
the same loadings,

00:55:05.640 --> 00:55:09.890 align:middle line:84%
those might actually
become correlated.

00:55:09.890 --> 00:55:14.210 align:middle line:84%
So here, I'm computing kind
of future returns projected

00:55:14.210 --> 00:55:18.790 align:middle line:84%
onto PC factors using data
calibrated on past history.

00:55:18.790 --> 00:55:23.890 align:middle line:84%
And you see that they're
not fully orthogonal.

00:55:23.890 --> 00:55:25.630 align:middle line:84%
However, they're
quite orthogonal.

00:55:25.630 --> 00:55:30.990 align:middle line:84%
So it means that the dynamics
are mostly preserved.

00:55:30.990 --> 00:55:34.690 align:middle line:84%
The PC factors
are mostly stable.

00:55:34.690 --> 00:55:37.593 align:middle line:84%
This is something you would
expect, you would hope to see.

00:55:37.593 --> 00:55:40.010 align:middle line:84%
You don't want to see these
numbers, off-diagonal numbers,

00:55:40.010 --> 00:55:44.530 align:middle line:84%
to become 30%-50%, that means
that your PCA model is quite

00:55:44.530 --> 00:55:45.110 align:middle line:90%
unstable.

00:55:45.110 --> 00:55:49.710 align:middle line:84%
But if your out-of-sample
correlation is pretty small,

00:55:49.710 --> 00:55:50.790 align:middle line:90%
that's a good thing.

00:55:50.790 --> 00:55:55.730 align:middle line:90%


00:55:55.730 --> 00:55:59.130 align:middle line:90%
Now what do we do with this PCA?

00:55:59.130 --> 00:56:03.050 align:middle line:90%
Well, one of the key things is--

00:56:03.050 --> 00:56:06.050 align:middle line:84%
I want to go back to this
slide for one reason.

00:56:06.050 --> 00:56:11.440 align:middle line:84%
Suppose you say, well,
I want to bet on curve--

00:56:11.440 --> 00:56:13.080 align:middle line:84%
I want to bet on
the second factor.

00:56:13.080 --> 00:56:16.220 align:middle line:84%
I have a model that I
want to bet on curves,

00:56:16.220 --> 00:56:19.460 align:middle line:84%
depending on relative value
of 10-year bonds versus 5-year

00:56:19.460 --> 00:56:23.380 align:middle line:84%
bonds or 30-year bonds
versus 10-year bonds.

00:56:23.380 --> 00:56:25.900 align:middle line:84%
I can use PCA to
isolate effects that

00:56:25.900 --> 00:56:29.100 align:middle line:84%
are idiosyncratic to
particular points on the curve

00:56:29.100 --> 00:56:30.940 align:middle line:90%
to particular factors.

00:56:30.940 --> 00:56:35.540 align:middle line:84%
And I can basically say,
well, if I hedge out

00:56:35.540 --> 00:56:40.060 align:middle line:84%
the vast majority of the
variance by hedging out PC1,

00:56:40.060 --> 00:56:44.180 align:middle line:84%
and I look at the residual
PC1 residuals, which

00:56:44.180 --> 00:56:51.200 align:middle line:84%
is PC2 and above, what can
I say about those returns?

00:56:51.200 --> 00:56:54.395 align:middle line:90%
How can I model those?

00:56:54.395 --> 00:56:56.520 align:middle line:84%
And the key thing-- you
can construct these things.

00:56:56.520 --> 00:56:58.437 align:middle line:84%
You can say, oh, I want
to model these things.

00:56:58.437 --> 00:57:01.940 align:middle line:84%
But the key is, once
you have a good model,

00:57:01.940 --> 00:57:03.380 align:middle line:90%
how do you trade them?

00:57:03.380 --> 00:57:05.650 align:middle line:90%
So being able to construct--

00:57:05.650 --> 00:57:08.710 align:middle line:84%
to understand how PC loadings
translate to portfolios

00:57:08.710 --> 00:57:11.390 align:middle line:84%
is very important
because it tells you

00:57:11.390 --> 00:57:14.870 align:middle line:84%
how I can use this
methodology to construct

00:57:14.870 --> 00:57:19.030 align:middle line:84%
specific portfolios that are
uncorrelated to each other

00:57:19.030 --> 00:57:27.550 align:middle line:84%
and give me a new kind
of target to model.

00:57:27.550 --> 00:57:30.630 align:middle line:84%
If I didn't do
that, I still have,

00:57:30.630 --> 00:57:33.350 align:middle line:84%
like, eight things I can
trade, or six things--

00:57:33.350 --> 00:57:36.110 align:middle line:84%
2-year, 3-year, 5-year,
10-year, and 30-year bonds.

00:57:36.110 --> 00:57:39.070 align:middle line:84%
But they're so correlated,
where I'm trading 10-year bonds

00:57:39.070 --> 00:57:43.030 align:middle line:84%
or 30-year bond, if I just trade
10-year bond outright or 30-year

00:57:43.030 --> 00:57:45.830 align:middle line:84%
bond outright, I'm mostly
getting the same return.

00:57:45.830 --> 00:57:49.710 align:middle line:84%
So to be able to increase the
diversity of the target I'm

00:57:49.710 --> 00:57:54.270 align:middle line:84%
capturing, I'm using PCA to
construct more portfolios that

00:57:54.270 --> 00:57:56.110 align:middle line:84%
are going to be
uncorrelated and present me

00:57:56.110 --> 00:58:00.230 align:middle line:84%
with more opportunities
to model, to make bets.

00:58:00.230 --> 00:58:03.270 align:middle line:84%
That's a key use case
for PCA in finance.

00:58:03.270 --> 00:58:05.830 align:middle line:84%
We take these things that
are very highly correlated.

00:58:05.830 --> 00:58:09.070 align:middle line:84%
And now I'm going to
create, out of these six

00:58:09.070 --> 00:58:10.470 align:middle line:84%
highly-correlated
portfolios, I'm

00:58:10.470 --> 00:58:12.990 align:middle line:84%
going to create three
or four portfolios that

00:58:12.990 --> 00:58:16.950 align:middle line:90%
are not highly correlated.

00:58:16.950 --> 00:58:22.390 align:middle line:84%
These portfolios are going to
be defined by my PCA factors.

00:58:22.390 --> 00:58:25.910 align:middle line:84%
So remember the
factors on any given

00:58:25.910 --> 00:58:31.030 align:middle line:84%
day, they give me a loading on
every tenure, on every maturity.

00:58:31.030 --> 00:58:32.110 align:middle line:90%
Did you have a question?

00:58:32.110 --> 00:58:32.670 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:58:32.670 --> 00:58:34.890 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, they give me a
loading on every maturity.

00:58:34.890 --> 00:58:38.150 align:middle line:84%
So it tells me-- and I
can think of this loading

00:58:38.150 --> 00:58:44.350 align:middle line:84%
as telling me how much to
own of this particular bond

00:58:44.350 --> 00:58:46.470 align:middle line:90%
on that maturity.

00:58:46.470 --> 00:58:52.670 align:middle line:84%
So PC1 means buy every
bond in some equal amount.

00:58:52.670 --> 00:58:53.870 align:middle line:90%
That's PC1.

00:58:53.870 --> 00:58:59.550 align:middle line:84%
PC2 is like, oh, you should
sell some to your bonds,

00:58:59.550 --> 00:59:01.720 align:middle line:90%
buy some 30-year bonds.

00:59:01.720 --> 00:59:05.280 align:middle line:84%
Basically, the portfolio
is going to be--

00:59:05.280 --> 00:59:07.760 align:middle line:84%
the portfolio is
going to be defined.

00:59:07.760 --> 00:59:12.480 align:middle line:84%
You're going to
basically buy bonds

00:59:12.480 --> 00:59:18.120 align:middle line:84%
according to the loading
on every point on PC2.

00:59:18.120 --> 00:59:19.780 align:middle line:84%
Why is this portfolio
interesting?

00:59:19.780 --> 00:59:22.680 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's interesting
because PCA tells us

00:59:22.680 --> 00:59:25.480 align:middle line:84%
that if I construct this
portfolio like this,

00:59:25.480 --> 00:59:28.960 align:middle line:84%
it would have no
sensitivity to PC1.

00:59:28.960 --> 00:59:32.220 align:middle line:84%
But PC1 is, like,
90% of my variance.

00:59:32.220 --> 00:59:36.000 align:middle line:84%
So that means that if I
construct the PC2 portfolio,

00:59:36.000 --> 00:59:39.680 align:middle line:84%
I have effectively hedged
out 90% of the variance

00:59:39.680 --> 00:59:41.178 align:middle line:90%
of the individual bonds.

00:59:41.178 --> 00:59:42.720 align:middle line:84%
And I've constructed
something that's

00:59:42.720 --> 00:59:48.960 align:middle line:84%
new, less volatile, but
uncorrelated to my original

00:59:48.960 --> 00:59:51.920 align:middle line:90%
outright tenure bond.

00:59:51.920 --> 00:59:54.720 align:middle line:90%
Same thing with PC3.

00:59:54.720 --> 00:59:58.040 align:middle line:84%
I can construct these
PC3 portfolios that are--

00:59:58.040 --> 01:00:00.830 align:middle line:84%
and by the way, these things
actually trade in the market.

01:00:00.830 --> 01:00:02.550 align:middle line:90%
They're called curves.

01:00:02.550 --> 01:00:05.650 align:middle line:90%
PC2 things are-- they're curves.

01:00:05.650 --> 01:00:09.590 align:middle line:84%
You buy 2-year, you sell
10-year, or vice versa.

01:00:09.590 --> 01:00:13.450 align:middle line:84%
And then there's 5's, which are
like 2's, 10's, and 30's, where

01:00:13.450 --> 01:00:16.650 align:middle line:84%
you sell 10-year and you
buy 2-year and 30-year.

01:00:16.650 --> 01:00:17.313 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:00:17.313 --> 01:00:18.730 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there
any application

01:00:18.730 --> 01:00:20.290 align:middle line:84%
of the higher-order
PCs or are they

01:00:20.290 --> 01:00:22.170 align:middle line:84%
just [INAUDIBLE]
up to the variance,

01:00:22.170 --> 01:00:23.570 align:middle line:90%
you don't really bother with?

01:00:23.570 --> 01:00:25.695 align:middle line:84%
STEFAN ANDREEV: They are
the most interesting thing

01:00:25.695 --> 01:00:30.090 align:middle line:84%
because the PC1 stuff
is easy to bet on.

01:00:30.090 --> 01:00:32.290 align:middle line:84%
You just buy 10-year
bond, buy and sell.

01:00:32.290 --> 01:00:37.330 align:middle line:84%
If you can model that and
predict it, that's awesome.

01:00:37.330 --> 01:00:40.090 align:middle line:90%
But first, that's hard.

01:00:40.090 --> 01:00:42.470 align:middle line:84%
But also, it's only one
thing you can predict.

01:00:42.470 --> 01:00:44.715 align:middle line:84%
What are you doing
10-year or 30-years?

01:00:44.715 --> 01:00:46.090 align:middle line:84%
You're predicting
only one thing,

01:00:46.090 --> 01:00:49.690 align:middle line:84%
so you can make
a model for that.

01:00:49.690 --> 01:00:53.210 align:middle line:84%
Being able to construct
these portfolios, PC2 and 3,

01:00:53.210 --> 01:00:58.400 align:middle line:84%
you're able to create,
out of these bonds that

01:00:58.400 --> 01:01:00.650 align:middle line:84%
are highly correlated, you're
able to create something

01:01:00.650 --> 01:01:03.770 align:middle line:84%
that is entirely
different from the PC1--

01:01:03.770 --> 01:01:04.930 align:middle line:90%
uncorrelated.

01:01:04.930 --> 01:01:08.590 align:middle line:84%
So now we have something
else that you can model.

01:01:08.590 --> 01:01:10.410 align:middle line:90%
And because of the market is--

01:01:10.410 --> 01:01:13.030 align:middle line:90%
so the market is liquid.

01:01:13.030 --> 01:01:15.490 align:middle line:84%
It needs to be very liquid
because, remember this,

01:01:15.490 --> 01:01:17.270 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't explain
a lot of variance.

01:01:17.270 --> 01:01:20.010 align:middle line:84%
So if you construct this thing,
it's not going to move much.

01:01:20.010 --> 01:01:23.250 align:middle line:84%
But all that means is I
just need to buy more of it.

01:01:23.250 --> 01:01:27.050 align:middle line:84%
If I buy enough of it, even
though a single unit might not

01:01:27.050 --> 01:01:29.110 align:middle line:84%
move much, when I
buy enough of it,

01:01:29.110 --> 01:01:30.450 align:middle line:90%
it's still going to move enough.

01:01:30.450 --> 01:01:32.770 align:middle line:84%
And that's the
point of leverage.

01:01:32.770 --> 01:01:35.210 align:middle line:84%
So if I sell a bunch
of 2-year bond,

01:01:35.210 --> 01:01:41.090 align:middle line:84%
buy a bunch of 30-year bond,
now if I buy the same notion

01:01:41.090 --> 01:01:44.370 align:middle line:84%
of-- buy and sell the
same notionals as the PC1,

01:01:44.370 --> 01:01:46.470 align:middle line:84%
it's not going
to, like you said,

01:01:46.470 --> 01:01:48.370 align:middle line:84%
it's not going to
move very much.

01:01:48.370 --> 01:01:50.450 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's going to
move only one third

01:01:50.450 --> 01:01:53.290 align:middle line:90%
as much as my PC1 portfolio.

01:01:53.290 --> 01:01:59.340 align:middle line:84%
But if I scale it up
by a factor of 3, now

01:01:59.340 --> 01:02:03.140 align:middle line:84%
my new portfolio, PC2, has the
same daily standard deviation

01:02:03.140 --> 01:02:05.540 align:middle line:90%
as my PC1 portfolio.

01:02:05.540 --> 01:02:06.720 align:middle line:90%
And it's orthogonal.

01:02:06.720 --> 01:02:08.883 align:middle line:84%
So you just need,
basically-- so what

01:02:08.883 --> 01:02:11.300 align:middle line:84%
did I need to do that I need
to be able to buy three times

01:02:11.300 --> 01:02:12.220 align:middle line:90%
as much?

01:02:12.220 --> 01:02:14.180 align:middle line:84%
So it needs the market
needs to be liquid.

01:02:14.180 --> 01:02:16.340 align:middle line:84%
I need to have
access to leverage.

01:02:16.340 --> 01:02:18.380 align:middle line:90%
You can do the same with PC3.

01:02:18.380 --> 01:02:22.280 align:middle line:84%
And yes, in the big liquid
markets, like US Treasury,

01:02:22.280 --> 01:02:24.060 align:middle line:84%
there is enough
access to leverage

01:02:24.060 --> 01:02:26.100 align:middle line:90%
where you can actually do that.

01:02:26.100 --> 01:02:27.940 align:middle line:84%
You will see a lot of
times in the press--

01:02:27.940 --> 01:02:30.020 align:middle line:90%
it's a very hot topic now.

01:02:30.020 --> 01:02:32.220 align:middle line:84%
The CFTC is trying to
regulate how much leverage

01:02:32.220 --> 01:02:36.870 align:middle line:84%
exists in the financial markets,
especially in interest rates.

01:02:36.870 --> 01:02:38.460 align:middle line:84%
Because of this,
because of the fact

01:02:38.460 --> 01:02:41.020 align:middle line:84%
that PC1 is so explains
so much, people

01:02:41.020 --> 01:02:43.180 align:middle line:84%
use massive amounts of
leverage to gain access

01:02:43.180 --> 01:02:49.340 align:middle line:84%
to, basically, enough volatility
to make these bets on PC2 and 3.

01:02:49.340 --> 01:02:53.840 align:middle line:84%
And that can create-- it
means you own large amount.

01:02:53.840 --> 01:02:57.870 align:middle line:84%
You own long and short, massive
amounts of certain bonds.

01:02:57.870 --> 01:03:00.650 align:middle line:84%
That can be dangerous if it's
systemic in a certain way,

01:03:00.650 --> 01:03:02.670 align:middle line:90%
and it presents risk.

01:03:02.670 --> 01:03:07.030 align:middle line:84%
But it's definitely doable
because there is so much debt.

01:03:07.030 --> 01:03:08.870 align:middle line:84%
There's so much
debt in the world

01:03:08.870 --> 01:03:13.787 align:middle line:84%
that there is enough out
there where you can bet--

01:03:13.787 --> 01:03:15.370 align:middle line:84%
you can gain this
leverage, basically.

01:03:15.370 --> 01:03:19.997 align:middle line:84%
And it's very accessible
to professional investors.

01:03:19.997 --> 01:03:20.830 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

01:03:20.830 --> 01:03:22.830 align:middle line:84%
STEFAN ANDREEV:
So yeah, actually,

01:03:22.830 --> 01:03:26.750 align:middle line:84%
far from it being
irrelevant, that

01:03:26.750 --> 01:03:29.910 align:middle line:84%
is actually where, a lot of
times, the money is made.

01:03:29.910 --> 01:03:35.190 align:middle line:84%
But it is true that as you go,
why can't we go to PC4 and 5?

01:03:35.190 --> 01:03:37.350 align:middle line:84%
The amount of leverage
you need to make this work

01:03:37.350 --> 01:03:41.670 align:middle line:84%
becomes so high it becomes
really expensive to trade.

01:03:41.670 --> 01:03:42.710 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:03:42.710 --> 01:03:45.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This is usually
[INAUDIBLE], as you said before,

01:03:45.410 --> 01:03:49.030 align:middle line:84%
that this allows you
where you can scale up

01:03:49.030 --> 01:03:51.750 align:middle line:84%
to get the volatility
that you need

01:03:51.750 --> 01:03:55.560 align:middle line:84%
is only useful for the
fixed income market

01:03:55.560 --> 01:03:58.840 align:middle line:84%
because it's more
liquid than if you're

01:03:58.840 --> 01:04:01.680 align:middle line:84%
applying the same thing
in equities, for example?

01:04:01.680 --> 01:04:04.680 align:middle line:84%
It's not as good,
but you could get--

01:04:04.680 --> 01:04:09.500 align:middle line:84%
if you were to buy that many
of whatever security costs,

01:04:09.500 --> 01:04:13.920 align:middle line:84%
too much of shift,
or stuff like that?

01:04:13.920 --> 01:04:17.560 align:middle line:84%
STEFAN ANDREEV:
Well, it turns out

01:04:17.560 --> 01:04:24.640 align:middle line:84%
that it's not really
true what you say,

01:04:24.640 --> 01:04:27.040 align:middle line:90%
and the reason is the following.

01:04:27.040 --> 01:04:30.520 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, the volumes and notional
volumes of equity market

01:04:30.520 --> 01:04:32.040 align:middle line:90%
may be a little bit smaller.

01:04:32.040 --> 01:04:33.680 align:middle line:90%
They're still very large.

01:04:33.680 --> 01:04:37.120 align:middle line:84%
And the variance explained of
the first principal component

01:04:37.120 --> 01:04:40.200 align:middle line:84%
equities is not nearly as
high as it is in rates.

01:04:40.200 --> 01:04:43.200 align:middle line:84%
So you don't need as
much leverage to get--

01:04:43.200 --> 01:04:45.940 align:middle line:84%
the second principal component
has got a lot of volatility,

01:04:45.940 --> 01:04:49.200 align:middle line:84%
a lot more relative to the
first, than in this example

01:04:49.200 --> 01:04:50.880 align:middle line:90%
here.

01:04:50.880 --> 01:04:53.700 align:middle line:84%
Later in the slides, which
I think we might not get to,

01:04:53.700 --> 01:04:57.200 align:middle line:84%
there is also an example with
equity indices in Europe--

01:04:57.200 --> 01:04:59.120 align:middle line:90%
the same concept there.

01:04:59.120 --> 01:05:00.523 align:middle line:84%
And by the way,
a lot of equity--

01:05:00.523 --> 01:05:02.440 align:middle line:84%
there is a whole field
of hedge funds equities

01:05:02.440 --> 01:05:05.240 align:middle line:84%
that are equity
long, short, neutral.

01:05:05.240 --> 01:05:08.320 align:middle line:84%
They are basically
betting on equities

01:05:08.320 --> 01:05:10.400 align:middle line:90%
without taking directional risk.

01:05:10.400 --> 01:05:11.820 align:middle line:90%
So everybody is trying--

01:05:11.820 --> 01:05:13.980 align:middle line:84%
all these guys are
hedging out PC1.

01:05:13.980 --> 01:05:15.400 align:middle line:90%
They're taking leverage.

01:05:15.400 --> 01:05:16.920 align:middle line:90%
It exists.

01:05:16.920 --> 01:05:18.840 align:middle line:84%
It's just a little more
expensive to trade,

01:05:18.840 --> 01:05:20.840 align:middle line:84%
but it gives you opportunities
that you wouldn't

01:05:20.840 --> 01:05:23.840 align:middle line:90%
be able to access otherwise.

01:05:23.840 --> 01:05:25.480 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:05:25.480 --> 01:05:27.773 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm curious for
some products, especially

01:05:27.773 --> 01:05:28.940 align:middle line:90%
ones that are more illiquid.

01:05:28.940 --> 01:05:35.280 align:middle line:84%
They might trade one, rarely,
and two, around specific events.

01:05:35.280 --> 01:05:39.080 align:middle line:84%
The prices that you
see are necessarily

01:05:39.080 --> 01:05:42.960 align:middle line:84%
indicative of how the
prices would have traded

01:05:42.960 --> 01:05:44.340 align:middle line:90%
a day or two before that?

01:05:44.340 --> 01:05:50.410 align:middle line:84%
And how do you think
about how to, I guess,

01:05:50.410 --> 01:05:53.850 align:middle line:84%
clean data, in a way, or
understand that there's

01:05:53.850 --> 01:05:55.330 align:middle line:84%
a lot of idiosyncratic
factors that

01:05:55.330 --> 01:05:57.690 align:middle line:84%
go on with fixing
the price, especially

01:05:57.690 --> 01:05:59.610 align:middle line:90%
ones that are more liquid?

01:05:59.610 --> 01:06:02.550 align:middle line:84%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Yeah,
it's a great question.

01:06:02.550 --> 01:06:06.570 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of art that
goes-- a lot of domain knowledge

01:06:06.570 --> 01:06:08.890 align:middle line:90%
that goes into that.

01:06:08.890 --> 01:06:12.570 align:middle line:84%
In this example with
bonds, yeah, there

01:06:12.570 --> 01:06:18.330 align:middle line:84%
are certainly events where
the market is very volatile.

01:06:18.330 --> 01:06:23.190 align:middle line:84%
And when we look
at daily returns,

01:06:23.190 --> 01:06:25.530 align:middle line:84%
if I look at 10 years
of daily returns

01:06:25.530 --> 01:06:28.730 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to estimate
a standard deviation,

01:06:28.730 --> 01:06:35.530 align:middle line:84%
and I'm going to say,
well, on average,

01:06:35.530 --> 01:06:39.610 align:middle line:84%
yields move by, I don't
know, 5 to 6 basis points

01:06:39.610 --> 01:06:42.250 align:middle line:84%
per day, something which
is-- that's roughly

01:06:42.250 --> 01:06:44.410 align:middle line:90%
how much they move.

01:06:44.410 --> 01:06:49.600 align:middle line:84%
And then if I'm sitting
there and tomorrow is like--

01:06:49.600 --> 01:06:55.460 align:middle line:84%
there was this example in
August that there was a huge--

01:06:55.460 --> 01:06:58.270 align:middle line:90%


01:06:58.270 --> 01:07:00.400 align:middle line:84%
there was a huge
number that comes out,

01:07:00.400 --> 01:07:04.380 align:middle line:84%
a huge print that was
related to the inflation.

01:07:04.380 --> 01:07:10.340 align:middle line:84%
And on that day, if you look
at the price of the day before

01:07:10.340 --> 01:07:13.100 align:middle line:84%
versus the price after
the announcement,

01:07:13.100 --> 01:07:15.980 align:middle line:84%
the volatility that people
expected on this day

01:07:15.980 --> 01:07:18.380 align:middle line:90%
was probably twice as high.

01:07:18.380 --> 01:07:21.780 align:middle line:84%
So people expected the standard
deviation of daily move

01:07:21.780 --> 01:07:26.160 align:middle line:84%
that was priced in was probably
10 to 12 basis points per day.

01:07:26.160 --> 01:07:29.540 align:middle line:90%


01:07:29.540 --> 01:07:32.020 align:middle line:84%
Does that mean that
all this is irrelevant?

01:07:32.020 --> 01:07:32.840 align:middle line:90%
No, it's not.

01:07:32.840 --> 01:07:35.900 align:middle line:84%
It just means that when you're
thinking about the risk you're

01:07:35.900 --> 01:07:39.180 align:middle line:84%
taking, you have to
understand that if you take

01:07:39.180 --> 01:07:43.620 align:middle line:84%
a certain amount of-- for
the same number of bonds

01:07:43.620 --> 01:07:47.740 align:middle line:84%
or the same number of the
same investment that you hold,

01:07:47.740 --> 01:07:49.340 align:middle line:84%
if you hold it on
that kind of a day,

01:07:49.340 --> 01:07:50.840 align:middle line:84%
you would just have
a lot more risk.

01:07:50.840 --> 01:07:53.380 align:middle line:84%
So when you're doing
your risk management,

01:07:53.380 --> 01:07:55.040 align:middle line:90%
you have to account for that.

01:07:55.040 --> 01:07:56.040 align:middle line:90%
Do you want to do that?

01:07:56.040 --> 01:07:56.998 align:middle line:90%
Do you want to hold it?

01:07:56.998 --> 01:07:58.900 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to maybe
shrink your position?

01:07:58.900 --> 01:08:00.220 align:middle line:84%
Or if you have a
view, maybe you don't

01:08:00.220 --> 01:08:01.440 align:middle line:90%
want to shrink your position.

01:08:01.440 --> 01:08:03.600 align:middle line:84%
But you have to
just be aware of it.

01:08:03.600 --> 01:08:06.320 align:middle line:84%
The correlations are very
likely to continue to hold.

01:08:06.320 --> 01:08:09.300 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes, things happen.

01:08:09.300 --> 01:08:10.040 align:middle line:90%
COVID happens.

01:08:10.040 --> 01:08:11.940 align:middle line:90%
For example, COVID hit.

01:08:11.940 --> 01:08:15.860 align:middle line:84%
These loadings, the dynamics of
the curve, changed overnight.

01:08:15.860 --> 01:08:21.130 align:middle line:84%
The PC loadings, the way the
curve started to behave--

01:08:21.130 --> 01:08:23.380 align:middle line:84%
the central bank came, and
there was an emergency cut,

01:08:23.380 --> 01:08:27.420 align:middle line:84%
and things changed
super quickly.

01:08:27.420 --> 01:08:29.473 align:middle line:84%
Being able to reflect
that in your model

01:08:29.473 --> 01:08:31.140 align:middle line:84%
is very important
because now, if you're

01:08:31.140 --> 01:08:35.540 align:middle line:84%
trying to take a PC2 bet,
but your PC loadings are not

01:08:35.540 --> 01:08:38.540 align:middle line:84%
indicative of how the
current dynamics are,

01:08:38.540 --> 01:08:43.370 align:middle line:84%
you're going to be ending
up taking massive leverage.

01:08:43.370 --> 01:08:45.430 align:middle line:84%
But you're not
really PC1 hedged,

01:08:45.430 --> 01:08:48.670 align:middle line:84%
so all this leverage is going to
produce huge amounts of returns

01:08:48.670 --> 01:08:54.229 align:middle line:84%
and noise in your P&L. So these
are the practical questions

01:08:54.229 --> 01:08:57.404 align:middle line:84%
that you face when you're
actually doing this thing.

01:08:57.404 --> 01:08:59.529 align:middle line:84%
And remember, you need
history to train your model.

01:08:59.529 --> 01:09:00.529 align:middle line:90%
So you're sitting there.

01:09:00.529 --> 01:09:01.470 align:middle line:90%
COVID happened.

01:09:01.470 --> 01:09:04.970 align:middle line:84%
It was a big cut, emergency
cut from the central bank.

01:09:04.970 --> 01:09:07.430 align:middle line:90%
They cut, like, 1% in one day.

01:09:07.430 --> 01:09:09.630 align:middle line:90%
And you know it's a new world.

01:09:09.630 --> 01:09:13.590 align:middle line:84%
But there is no data
on how the new world is

01:09:13.590 --> 01:09:15.850 align:middle line:84%
going to behave because it
just happened two days ago.

01:09:15.850 --> 01:09:17.267 align:middle line:84%
You don't have 10
years of history

01:09:17.267 --> 01:09:19.069 align:middle line:90%
to calibrate your PCA model.

01:09:19.069 --> 01:09:20.910 align:middle line:90%
So what are you going to do?

01:09:20.910 --> 01:09:22.490 align:middle line:84%
And there's a lot
of answers to that.

01:09:22.490 --> 01:09:23.850 align:middle line:90%
You can look at implied markets.

01:09:23.850 --> 01:09:25.609 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of
answers to that.

01:09:25.609 --> 01:09:30.189 align:middle line:84%
But how you answer that
question, that's critical.

01:09:30.189 --> 01:09:32.833 align:middle line:90%
That's critical, yeah.

01:09:32.833 --> 01:09:34.250 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can I
follow up to that?

01:09:34.250 --> 01:09:34.710 align:middle line:90%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Yeah.

01:09:34.710 --> 01:09:36.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do you
think it's worth,

01:09:36.330 --> 01:09:40.920 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, almost slicing
your data into pieces where you

01:09:40.920 --> 01:09:45.520 align:middle line:84%
think things will behave
in similar fashions,

01:09:45.520 --> 01:09:49.359 align:middle line:84%
and then, like, you say
something, a similar model,

01:09:49.359 --> 01:09:53.160 align:middle line:84%
to better understand how they
behave in those specific regimes

01:09:53.160 --> 01:09:54.960 align:middle line:90%
or even times?

01:09:54.960 --> 01:09:57.480 align:middle line:84%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Yeah, that's
certainly a thing that that is

01:09:57.480 --> 01:10:00.600 align:middle line:84%
a thing that you can do,
like regime-specific--

01:10:00.600 --> 01:10:03.920 align:middle line:84%
you can group together data that
are specific to this regime.

01:10:03.920 --> 01:10:06.620 align:middle line:84%
Oh, I'm going to look at, right
now, the Fed is cutting rates.

01:10:06.620 --> 01:10:09.400 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to model
my curve behavior not

01:10:09.400 --> 01:10:12.080 align:middle line:84%
by looking at the last
five years of history,

01:10:12.080 --> 01:10:15.280 align:middle line:84%
but I'm going to look
at joined together

01:10:15.280 --> 01:10:17.640 align:middle line:84%
historical periods where
the Fed was cutting rates

01:10:17.640 --> 01:10:19.040 align:middle line:90%
and just look at that.

01:10:19.040 --> 01:10:22.360 align:middle line:84%
It's an approach, and
it's a sensible approach.

01:10:22.360 --> 01:10:25.425 align:middle line:84%
Now, when you do that,
you're introducing other--

01:10:25.425 --> 01:10:27.800 align:middle line:84%
yes, the Fed is cutting rates,
but there are other things

01:10:27.800 --> 01:10:28.820 align:middle line:90%
that were not the same.

01:10:28.820 --> 01:10:31.760 align:middle line:84%
So there's no perfect
answer in that

01:10:31.760 --> 01:10:34.120 align:middle line:84%
because, again, you're
still trying to use the past

01:10:34.120 --> 01:10:35.060 align:middle line:90%
to predict the future.

01:10:35.060 --> 01:10:37.980 align:middle line:90%
And there is no perfect answer.

01:10:37.980 --> 01:10:39.660 align:middle line:84%
But there are many
valid answers.

01:10:39.660 --> 01:10:48.170 align:middle line:90%


01:10:48.170 --> 01:10:51.450 align:middle line:84%
This is a description of how you
construct these PC portfolios

01:10:51.450 --> 01:10:53.530 align:middle line:90%
and PC residuals.

01:10:53.530 --> 01:10:55.650 align:middle line:84%
We have some of
the pros and cons.

01:10:55.650 --> 01:10:59.890 align:middle line:84%
I already talked
about some of these.

01:10:59.890 --> 01:11:07.950 align:middle line:84%
And I kind of wanted to talk
about the output of this.

01:11:07.950 --> 01:11:11.330 align:middle line:84%
So here, I've created a
little trading strategy

01:11:11.330 --> 01:11:17.050 align:middle line:90%
based on the returns of--

01:11:17.050 --> 01:11:22.490 align:middle line:84%
basically, a momentum mean
reversion strategy for PC1

01:11:22.490 --> 01:11:24.298 align:middle line:90%
and looked at the performance.

01:11:24.298 --> 01:11:26.590 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't really work great,
but that's not the point.

01:11:26.590 --> 01:11:29.690 align:middle line:84%
The point is that it's an
illustration of how this works.

01:11:29.690 --> 01:11:32.050 align:middle line:84%
All the code of how
this was created

01:11:32.050 --> 01:11:33.870 align:middle line:84%
and how you could
calculate these things

01:11:33.870 --> 01:11:38.450 align:middle line:84%
is part of that Jupyter Notebook
that is accompanying the slides

01:11:38.450 --> 01:11:39.430 align:middle line:90%
that you can download.

01:11:39.430 --> 01:11:42.930 align:middle line:84%
And the data is there
in a CSV file, which

01:11:42.930 --> 01:11:44.450 align:middle line:90%
you can pull from the Fed--

01:11:44.450 --> 01:11:46.090 align:middle line:90%
an updated version of.

01:11:46.090 --> 01:11:50.790 align:middle line:84%
So if you are interested
in doing a project,

01:11:50.790 --> 01:11:53.770 align:middle line:84%
there's a lot of
standard strategies

01:11:53.770 --> 01:12:01.383 align:middle line:84%
that you can try, for example,
to write and to test out.

01:12:01.383 --> 01:12:03.550 align:middle line:84%
Again, the point is not to
actually go and trade it,

01:12:03.550 --> 01:12:06.890 align:middle line:84%
but to actually
practice a little bit,

01:12:06.890 --> 01:12:08.630 align:middle line:90%
applying PCA to real data.

01:12:08.630 --> 01:12:11.970 align:middle line:84%
And there's no substitute
to actually doing it

01:12:11.970 --> 01:12:14.970 align:middle line:84%
because you see that,
in every single step,

01:12:14.970 --> 01:12:15.972 align:middle line:90%
there's choices to make.

01:12:15.972 --> 01:12:16.930 align:middle line:90%
There's little details.

01:12:16.930 --> 01:12:19.050 align:middle line:84%
And these details
matter and being

01:12:19.050 --> 01:12:24.690 align:middle line:84%
able to understand the pros and
cons of each of these details.

01:12:24.690 --> 01:12:28.010 align:middle line:84%
One lecture is not enough
to go over everything.

01:12:28.010 --> 01:12:30.330 align:middle line:84%
I've wanted to give you
a little taste of what

01:12:30.330 --> 01:12:32.350 align:middle line:84%
it's like to deal
with this stuff,

01:12:32.350 --> 01:12:34.190 align:middle line:90%
but it's a very powerful tool.

01:12:34.190 --> 01:12:39.540 align:middle line:84%
It's used all around the
street for modeling of things

01:12:39.540 --> 01:12:41.860 align:middle line:90%
like stocks and bonds.

01:12:41.860 --> 01:12:47.420 align:middle line:84%
And it's a great thing to have
in your toolkit as somebody who

01:12:47.420 --> 01:12:50.920 align:middle line:84%
is looking for a job in finance,
if you decide to do that.

01:12:50.920 --> 01:12:53.700 align:middle line:90%


01:12:53.700 --> 01:12:55.280 align:middle line:90%
So I'm going to stop here.

01:12:55.280 --> 01:12:57.560 align:middle line:84%
Down here, there
is another example,

01:12:57.560 --> 01:12:59.660 align:middle line:84%
which is European
stock market indices.

01:12:59.660 --> 01:13:02.700 align:middle line:84%
Same thing-- tells you
about the correlation, what

01:13:02.700 --> 01:13:06.140 align:middle line:84%
the various principal
components are, what they mean,

01:13:06.140 --> 01:13:08.240 align:middle line:84%
and tells you
about the strategy.

01:13:08.240 --> 01:13:12.100 align:middle line:84%
So there's two
examples in the slides.

01:13:12.100 --> 01:13:14.700 align:middle line:84%
We don't need to go
through both of them.

01:13:14.700 --> 01:13:17.440 align:middle line:84%
I know that, in five
minutes, you have to leave.

01:13:17.440 --> 01:13:22.980 align:middle line:84%
So I wanted to give
a little bit of time

01:13:22.980 --> 01:13:26.300 align:middle line:84%
for questions and
for discussion.

01:13:26.300 --> 01:13:32.630 align:middle line:84%
Thank you so much for being so
active and interactive, rather.

01:13:32.630 --> 01:13:34.230 align:middle line:90%
You guys have.

01:13:34.230 --> 01:13:37.430 align:middle line:84%
You guys have asked more
questions in this class than--

01:13:37.430 --> 01:13:42.030 align:middle line:84%
it's like definitely one of
the top question-asking classes

01:13:42.030 --> 01:13:44.850 align:middle line:84%
in my experience
doing this class.

01:13:44.850 --> 01:13:46.800 align:middle line:90%
So I love that.

01:13:46.800 --> 01:13:49.370 align:middle line:84%
That shows that the
stuff is interesting,

01:13:49.370 --> 01:13:50.770 align:middle line:90%
so I'm very happy to hear that.

01:13:50.770 --> 01:13:53.390 align:middle line:90%


01:13:53.390 --> 01:13:58.250 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, I definitely wanted to
ask if you have any questions.

01:13:58.250 --> 01:14:02.210 align:middle line:84%
And in particular,
if this is clear,

01:14:02.210 --> 01:14:04.990 align:middle line:84%
I really want to make sure
that this stuff is clear--

01:14:04.990 --> 01:14:08.690 align:middle line:84%
these top three plots,
what they represent.

01:14:08.690 --> 01:14:09.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:14:09.190 --> 01:14:10.815 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there
any chance you could

01:14:10.815 --> 01:14:13.295 align:middle line:90%
explain the bottom left one?

01:14:13.295 --> 01:14:14.170 align:middle line:90%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Yeah.

01:14:14.170 --> 01:14:21.190 align:middle line:84%
So the bottom left
one, basically.

01:14:21.190 --> 01:14:28.050 align:middle line:84%
So if there's two PC1
shapes, this is date 1,

01:14:28.050 --> 01:14:32.320 align:middle line:90%
and there is one that is date 2.

01:14:32.320 --> 01:14:43.560 align:middle line:84%
I kind of said, well, what I'm
looking for is basically the--

01:14:43.560 --> 01:14:47.080 align:middle line:84%
I'm looking for
the maturity point

01:14:47.080 --> 01:14:51.720 align:middle line:84%
where the loadings cross
a certain threshold

01:14:51.720 --> 01:14:53.383 align:middle line:84%
because, at the
end, both of these

01:14:53.383 --> 01:14:54.800 align:middle line:84%
are going to be
kind of relatively

01:14:54.800 --> 01:14:56.180 align:middle line:90%
flat towards the back end.

01:14:56.180 --> 01:14:58.300 align:middle line:84%
So basically, 10-year
bonds and 30-year bonds,

01:14:58.300 --> 01:15:00.320 align:middle line:84%
when the rates move up and down,
they're going to move the same

01:15:00.320 --> 01:15:01.160 align:middle line:90%
amount.

01:15:01.160 --> 01:15:03.300 align:middle line:84%
But on the very front end,
that's not really true.

01:15:03.300 --> 01:15:04.060 align:middle line:90%
And why is that?

01:15:04.060 --> 01:15:06.640 align:middle line:84%
Well, on the very
front end, the rates

01:15:06.640 --> 01:15:08.020 align:middle line:84%
are governed by
the central bank.

01:15:08.020 --> 01:15:12.480 align:middle line:84%
So central bank is saying, we're
going to set the rates here.

01:15:12.480 --> 01:15:15.320 align:middle line:84%
Expectations can vary for what
happens 10 years down the line.

01:15:15.320 --> 01:15:17.320 align:middle line:84%
But expectations are
what's going to happen over

01:15:17.320 --> 01:15:19.640 align:middle line:84%
the next couple
of months are not

01:15:19.640 --> 01:15:22.360 align:middle line:84%
going to vary very much
because that is basically--

01:15:22.360 --> 01:15:24.560 align:middle line:90%
the Fed sets that.

01:15:24.560 --> 01:15:27.240 align:middle line:84%
The Fed tells you what
is going to happen.

01:15:27.240 --> 01:15:32.400 align:middle line:90%
So the loading of this PC1--

01:15:32.400 --> 01:15:35.480 align:middle line:84%
that front point is much
less volatile in general.

01:15:35.480 --> 01:15:36.440 align:middle line:90%
That can change.

01:15:36.440 --> 01:15:39.600 align:middle line:84%
It could also be that in the
recent-- in the last couple

01:15:39.600 --> 01:15:44.080 align:middle line:84%
of months, the actual
shape of the PC1 factor

01:15:44.080 --> 01:15:45.620 align:middle line:90%
was probably like that.

01:15:45.620 --> 01:15:48.080 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is that the
front end was so uncertain.

01:15:48.080 --> 01:15:49.023 align:middle line:90%
Nobody knew.

01:15:49.023 --> 01:15:49.940 align:middle line:90%
Are they going to cut?

01:15:49.940 --> 01:15:50.898 align:middle line:90%
Are they going to hike?

01:15:50.898 --> 01:15:53.440 align:middle line:84%
We don't really know
what's going to happen.

01:15:53.440 --> 01:15:55.920 align:middle line:84%
But generally
speaking, the front end

01:15:55.920 --> 01:16:00.320 align:middle line:84%
is going to be less volatile
than the back end because

01:16:00.320 --> 01:16:06.560 align:middle line:84%
of the fact that the
Federal Reserve--

01:16:06.560 --> 01:16:10.600 align:middle line:84%
they control the front
end part of the curve.

01:16:10.600 --> 01:16:15.360 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm plotting is,
effectively, at what point does

01:16:15.360 --> 01:16:18.400 align:middle line:90%
the loadings cross a threshold?

01:16:18.400 --> 01:16:21.900 align:middle line:84%
And if the Fed
controls the curve,

01:16:21.900 --> 01:16:24.960 align:middle line:84%
if people believe the
Fed is going to have--

01:16:24.960 --> 01:16:27.730 align:middle line:84%
if there's clarity on the
Fed's policy over a longer

01:16:27.730 --> 01:16:31.890 align:middle line:84%
period of time, we're going
to have lower loadings

01:16:31.890 --> 01:16:35.050 align:middle line:84%
on the front of the curve
for longer periods of time.

01:16:35.050 --> 01:16:38.350 align:middle line:84%
And if there is less clarity,
that period is going to shrink,

01:16:38.350 --> 01:16:41.150 align:middle line:84%
meaning, yeah, they're always
going to control the front end.

01:16:41.150 --> 01:16:45.170 align:middle line:84%
But how quickly do we
decouple from that?

01:16:45.170 --> 01:16:47.170 align:middle line:84%
And that's what
I'm plotting here.

01:16:47.170 --> 01:16:49.470 align:middle line:84%
On every day, I'm
taking this point.

01:16:49.470 --> 01:16:50.270 align:middle line:90%
This is on day one.

01:16:50.270 --> 01:16:51.690 align:middle line:90%
It's going to be here.

01:16:51.690 --> 01:16:53.630 align:middle line:84%
On some other day,
it's going to be here.

01:16:53.630 --> 01:16:57.050 align:middle line:84%
And I'm plotting a time
series of that point.

01:16:57.050 --> 01:17:01.050 align:middle line:84%
At what time in maturity
years, at which maturity

01:17:01.050 --> 01:17:03.503 align:middle line:90%
do we cross the threshold?

01:17:03.503 --> 01:17:05.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can you
explain the connection

01:17:05.170 --> 01:17:08.465 align:middle line:84%
between the curvature and the
macroeconomics situation there?

01:17:08.465 --> 01:17:10.090 align:middle line:84%
STEFAN ANDREEV: The
curvature and what?

01:17:10.090 --> 01:17:11.848 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The
macroeconomics, like.

01:17:11.848 --> 01:17:12.890 align:middle line:90%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Ah, yeah.

01:17:12.890 --> 01:17:15.910 align:middle line:90%


01:17:15.910 --> 01:17:19.490 align:middle line:84%
So the PC3 shape,
remember, was like--

01:17:19.490 --> 01:17:22.770 align:middle line:90%
this is 0.

01:17:22.770 --> 01:17:26.860 align:middle line:84%
And it's positive,
negative, positive.

01:17:26.860 --> 01:17:30.740 align:middle line:84%
So the short end and
the back end are--

01:17:30.740 --> 01:17:37.260 align:middle line:84%
basically, once you hedge out,
the curvature and the levels,

01:17:37.260 --> 01:17:38.140 align:middle line:90%
what remains is--

01:17:38.140 --> 01:17:41.580 align:middle line:90%


01:17:41.580 --> 01:17:43.860 align:middle line:84%
once you hedge out the slope
and levels, what remains

01:17:43.860 --> 01:17:44.680 align:middle line:90%
is the curvature.

01:17:44.680 --> 01:17:51.020 align:middle line:84%
And the question is, at that
point, how does the curve move?

01:17:51.020 --> 01:17:53.540 align:middle line:90%
And I can give you two examples.

01:17:53.540 --> 01:17:56.340 align:middle line:84%
In dollars, you can see
that most of the time,

01:17:56.340 --> 01:18:00.320 align:middle line:84%
the bottom of this graph is
at around 10-year maturity.

01:18:00.320 --> 01:18:04.460 align:middle line:84%
So that means that once you
hedge out slope and curvature,

01:18:04.460 --> 01:18:07.540 align:middle line:84%
10-year bonds are going to
be anticorrelated with 2-year

01:18:07.540 --> 01:18:08.995 align:middle line:90%
and 30-year bonds.

01:18:08.995 --> 01:18:10.620 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to move
around like that,

01:18:10.620 --> 01:18:14.940 align:middle line:84%
with where the belly of
the curve is 10-year point.

01:18:14.940 --> 01:18:18.140 align:middle line:84%
Now if I look at
another country that

01:18:18.140 --> 01:18:20.760 align:middle line:84%
is more like an emerging
markets country, for example,

01:18:20.760 --> 01:18:27.020 align:middle line:84%
like Mexico, it's going to
look like this, where this is

01:18:27.020 --> 01:18:30.700 align:middle line:90%
going to be around five years.

01:18:30.700 --> 01:18:32.740 align:middle line:84%
What is the
significance of this?

01:18:32.740 --> 01:18:35.420 align:middle line:90%
Well, it tells you--

01:18:35.420 --> 01:18:39.900 align:middle line:84%
you can think of this as the
curve has distinct parts.

01:18:39.900 --> 01:18:41.980 align:middle line:90%
Different things drive it.

01:18:41.980 --> 01:18:44.700 align:middle line:84%
The very front end, we
say, is the central bank.

01:18:44.700 --> 01:18:49.780 align:middle line:84%
Then there's a period after
that is really mostly driven

01:18:49.780 --> 01:18:52.060 align:middle line:90%
by inflation expectations.

01:18:52.060 --> 01:18:54.060 align:middle line:84%
And then the very long
end is a lot of it

01:18:54.060 --> 01:18:57.860 align:middle line:90%
is about growth expectations.

01:18:57.860 --> 01:19:04.780 align:middle line:84%
And the point where this
bottom of the curvature

01:19:04.780 --> 01:19:10.820 align:middle line:84%
is, it really tells you a lot
about what part of the curve

01:19:10.820 --> 01:19:11.360 align:middle line:90%
is--

01:19:11.360 --> 01:19:14.500 align:middle line:84%
where does this crossover
go from inflation

01:19:14.500 --> 01:19:17.340 align:middle line:84%
being dominant to
growth being dominant?

01:19:17.340 --> 01:19:22.293 align:middle line:84%
Different players invest in
different parts of the curve.

01:19:22.293 --> 01:19:23.710 align:middle line:84%
The back end of
the curve, there's

01:19:23.710 --> 01:19:26.230 align:middle line:84%
a lot of pensions, insurance
companies investing

01:19:26.230 --> 01:19:27.170 align:middle line:90%
in the very long end.

01:19:27.170 --> 01:19:28.870 align:middle line:90%
They have long-term liabilities.

01:19:28.870 --> 01:19:30.010 align:middle line:90%
They invest here.

01:19:30.010 --> 01:19:34.570 align:middle line:84%
Very front end is a
lot of 2-year rates,

01:19:34.570 --> 01:19:38.510 align:middle line:84%
a lot of speculators, a lot
of people who need short-term

01:19:38.510 --> 01:19:39.710 align:middle line:90%
financing.

01:19:39.710 --> 01:19:44.790 align:middle line:84%
10-year in the US, between 5 and
10 years is where most corporate

01:19:44.790 --> 01:19:46.650 align:middle line:84%
bonds, most
corporations they issue,

01:19:46.650 --> 01:19:49.150 align:middle line:84%
they raise debt in the
5 to 10-year maturity.

01:19:49.150 --> 01:19:53.790 align:middle line:84%
So different players participate
in the different parts

01:19:53.790 --> 01:19:54.950 align:middle line:90%
of the curve.

01:19:54.950 --> 01:19:59.463 align:middle line:84%
And that means the
dynamics are different.

01:19:59.463 --> 01:20:01.130 align:middle line:84%
Once you hedge out
those two components,

01:20:01.130 --> 01:20:02.750 align:middle line:90%
you start to discover those--

01:20:02.750 --> 01:20:04.970 align:middle line:84%
these dynamics are small,
relatively speaking,

01:20:04.970 --> 01:20:07.450 align:middle line:84%
on the scale of rates
going up and down.

01:20:07.450 --> 01:20:09.510 align:middle line:84%
But once you remove
that, you start

01:20:09.510 --> 01:20:10.770 align:middle line:90%
to discover these features.

01:20:10.770 --> 01:20:14.070 align:middle line:84%
They're kind of hidden
under this big noise

01:20:14.070 --> 01:20:16.270 align:middle line:90%
of rates going up and down.

01:20:16.270 --> 01:20:18.850 align:middle line:84%
And you start to see these
things start to pop out.

01:20:18.850 --> 01:20:21.840 align:middle line:90%


01:20:21.840 --> 01:20:25.600 align:middle line:84%
This being longer term, it tells
you, again, something about--

01:20:25.600 --> 01:20:28.200 align:middle line:84%
developed markets tend
to have this belly be

01:20:28.200 --> 01:20:33.960 align:middle line:84%
further out because people
look at a longer-term horizon,

01:20:33.960 --> 01:20:36.240 align:middle line:90%
essentially, for investing.

01:20:36.240 --> 01:20:38.340 align:middle line:84%
The pension funds
invest for longer.

01:20:38.340 --> 01:20:40.800 align:middle line:90%
They buy longer-term bonds.

01:20:40.800 --> 01:20:44.940 align:middle line:84%
And generally, people invest
in longer term than in, say,

01:20:44.940 --> 01:20:47.400 align:middle line:90%
emerging market countries.

01:20:47.400 --> 01:20:54.000 align:middle line:84%
So when you compare these
factors across countries,

01:20:54.000 --> 01:20:56.740 align:middle line:84%
you can actually make sense
of some of these things.

01:20:56.740 --> 01:21:01.320 align:middle line:84%
And you can quantify
those things,

01:21:01.320 --> 01:21:03.070 align:middle line:84%
and they can become
features for modeling.

01:21:03.070 --> 01:21:08.440 align:middle line:90%


01:21:08.440 --> 01:21:11.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: One more
question, perhaps?

01:21:11.640 --> 01:21:12.958 align:middle line:90%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Yeah.

01:21:12.958 --> 01:21:14.500 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: For a lot
of these markets,

01:21:14.500 --> 01:21:16.083 align:middle line:84%
you're not going to
get prices equally

01:21:16.083 --> 01:21:18.530 align:middle line:90%
for something like US Treasury.

01:21:18.530 --> 01:21:21.970 align:middle line:90%
Is there a way in which by--

01:21:21.970 --> 01:21:23.970 align:middle line:84%
trying to ask for
transactional prices,

01:21:23.970 --> 01:21:28.050 align:middle line:84%
do a synthetic [INAUDIBLE] with
data [INAUDIBLE] would have?

01:21:28.050 --> 01:21:31.010 align:middle line:84%
STEFAN ANDREEV: Yeah,
getting access to good data

01:21:31.010 --> 01:21:35.570 align:middle line:84%
is step one in every
quantitative finance project.

01:21:35.570 --> 01:21:39.510 align:middle line:84%
I would say that
that may be true,

01:21:39.510 --> 01:21:42.470 align:middle line:90%
but there's ways to get it.

01:21:42.470 --> 01:21:45.970 align:middle line:84%
First of all, there's bond
futures, which are effectively

01:21:45.970 --> 01:21:47.230 align:middle line:90%
futures on bonds.

01:21:47.230 --> 01:21:48.550 align:middle line:90%
Those trade on exchanges.

01:21:48.550 --> 01:21:50.970 align:middle line:84%
The data also tends to be
pretty high quality and readily

01:21:50.970 --> 01:21:51.590 align:middle line:90%
available.

01:21:51.590 --> 01:21:53.070 align:middle line:90%
So there's ways to get it.

01:21:53.070 --> 01:21:57.770 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, for every
particular market you look at,

01:21:57.770 --> 01:22:00.050 align:middle line:84%
there's going to be
certain data quality

01:22:00.050 --> 01:22:03.010 align:middle line:90%
issues you have to overcome.

01:22:03.010 --> 01:22:05.588 align:middle line:84%
US market is the
easiest for that.

01:22:05.588 --> 01:22:07.130 align:middle line:84%
Now it's part of,
for example, that's

01:22:07.130 --> 01:22:09.297 align:middle line:84%
why for example, if you're
going to do your project,

01:22:09.297 --> 01:22:11.570 align:middle line:84%
you're not going to look
at South African rates

01:22:11.570 --> 01:22:14.010 align:middle line:84%
because it's just
hard to get good data.

01:22:14.010 --> 01:22:16.690 align:middle line:84%
But if you're in a hedge
fund, maybe, oh, it's

01:22:16.690 --> 01:22:18.690 align:middle line:84%
worth getting the data
and putting some effort

01:22:18.690 --> 01:22:21.070 align:middle line:84%
because maybe you have
less competition there.

01:22:21.070 --> 01:22:22.590 align:middle line:90%
You can maybe make some money.

01:22:22.590 --> 01:22:25.170 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe you have some ideas
where you could model it,

01:22:25.170 --> 01:22:27.290 align:middle line:84%
where you can actually
have some predictive power

01:22:27.290 --> 01:22:28.390 align:middle line:90%
in South African rates.

01:22:28.390 --> 01:22:30.930 align:middle line:84%
So you make that
investment of time.

01:22:30.930 --> 01:22:33.200 align:middle line:90%
But it's all part of that.

01:22:33.200 --> 01:22:36.290 align:middle line:90%


01:22:36.290 --> 01:22:38.410 align:middle line:84%
The harder it is to
get the data usually

01:22:38.410 --> 01:22:39.850 align:middle line:84%
means that there's
probably going

01:22:39.850 --> 01:22:42.290 align:middle line:84%
to be more opportunities
for quantitative modeling

01:22:42.290 --> 01:22:44.030 align:middle line:90%
because it's hard for everybody.

01:22:44.030 --> 01:22:45.710 align:middle line:84%
If it's easy, then
everybody can do it.

01:22:45.710 --> 01:22:54.768 align:middle line:84%
So you always have to be on the
edge of the wave to make money.

01:22:54.768 --> 01:22:55.310 align:middle line:90%
PETER: Great.

01:22:55.310 --> 01:22:56.810 align:middle line:90%
Thank you so much.

01:22:56.810 --> 01:22:58.480 align:middle line:90%
STEFAN ANDREEV: You're welcome.

01:22:58.480 --> 01:23:14.000 align:middle line:90%